window.harnessChunks["rows-dc832b87e650b45a.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T19:10:04Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/henrik-me/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Henrik Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/henrik-me/agent-harness/tree/9168fa451b69c5d53b961ab5c805b3067597ade4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9168fa451b69c5d53b961ab5c805b3067597ade4\">9168fa4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce substantive bounded changes and reviews in consumer software work through distinct model-backed contributors, and source-connected Copilot PR analysis. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-specific source/edit choices, unexpected actual code state, review findings and failure/repair feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In the supported model-operated consumer mode, each contributor model chooses content of its scoped implementation or critique; GitHub Copilot chooses PR review findings from the actual current PR head. - Decision owner: Externally provided model agents acting under first-party scoped briefing and PR-review invocation; human/operator chooses the task and final merge, not the agent&#39;s substantive code/review response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness dispatch emitted canonical prompt and owned-file scope, WORKBOARD.md, clickstop plan, real GitHub PRs, reviewer model allowlists and managed review gate checks. - Closure path: Consumer work/PR requirement → orchestrator supplies bounded first-party briefing or CLI requests Copilot reviewer → model performs implementation or PR review → changed files/review findings returned to the consumer PR/workboard → further correction and submission. - Boundary reachability: harness init/sync installs the model-enabled process instructions; harness review explicitly performs the GitHub reviewer request/poll rather than only drawing an agent diagram. External agent host/Copilot authorization must be configured; CLI-only mode has no model executor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed specialists and reviewer choose substantive decisions; deterministic Node tooling cannot derive their implementation or review findings without the model. This is an A mode only in the declared connected model-operated consumer cell, not for a standalone Node CLI. - Evidence: model roles and parallel work, subagent briefing and output, review execution path, real Copilot review request/poll. - Basis: First-party consumer operating contract plus executable external model reviewer integration. - Confidence: Medium source-structural; distinct implementer execution depends on external agent host not bundled. - Caveats: GitHub reviewer credentials, model quality, independent coding success and a complete live adopter project were not demonstrated. No autonomous execution claimed for the CLI itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent destructive conflicts among parallel operational contributors and overlapping currently active work claims before they affect other S1 outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent specialists silently overwriting one another&#39;s files and two orchestrator work claims racing for an already occupied coordination slot. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Establish disjoint per-agent editable file scopes and disallow a second active work claim for one owner unless deliberately transferred/taken over. - Decision owner: The parent orchestrator or operator decides actual task partition/scopes and takeovers; first-party deterministic CLI state and briefing constructors enforce/show preconditions, not autonomous mutual compromise. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit owned/read-only/do-not-touch briefing fields, no-commit worker discipline, WORKBOARD ownership rows, apply-time re-read/refusal, scoped clickstop branches. - Closure path: Parent receives parallel task candidates → declares separate file ownership in each emitted brief and claims a single active work slot → worker follows scoped briefing; conflicting claim is rejected, requiring reassignment before additional work proceeds → subsequent agent tasks operate on revised or nonoverlapping assignments. - Boundary reachability: harness dispatch builds emitted own/do-not-touch sections on every configured dispatch; claim directly checks the current WORKBOARD at plan and mutation time and fails closed for active owner collisions. These are delivered consumer-facing CLI commands and managed policy, not an aspirational slide. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concrete inter-worker lost-edit failure is documented in the source policy, and S2-specific interference is regulated through source-wired constructor/enforcement. The external orchestrator must pick the compromise, and the CLI does not independently detect all overlaps between distinct work files/agents. - Distinct S1 units: Independently model-operated specialist implementers assigned different concurrently active clickstop work and file scopes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two contributors can write one overlapping file and silently lose the earlier changes; the process source describes an observed lib/templating.mjs / lib/lock.mjs collision from parallel dispatch. - Attenuating coordination relation: Allocate exclusive writable sets per specialist and a single active work claim per owner; reject owner collision at apply-time. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A conflicting owner work claim fails or a shared file becomes explicitly read-only for one worker; parent must rebrief/serialize/reclaim before launching the affected worker. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: These targeted mechanisms mitigate a stated inter-contributor lost-edit race and concurrent work-claim collision. Generic passing of a task ID or state alone is not counted. - Evidence: actual lost-edit incident and explicit file ownership, briefing file scope, claim collision check, apply-time recheck. - Basis: Shipped S2-specific coordination constructor with source-documented disturbance. - Confidence: Medium-high for the narrow fail-closed work-claim check and explicit overlapping-file procedure. - Caveats: The emitted brief does not compare all concurrent file scope declarations mechanically; adherence depends on the actual external orchestrator/worker.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Assemble a whole-current delivery view and a bounded work-commitment lifecycle, allowing an external management actor to choose and revise active staffing, priorities and release/rework intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing active clickstops, stale work, incomplete handoffs, unidentified worker ownership, current blocked dependencies and inconsistent PR closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which planned clickstop to activate, assign its owner/branch, accept a failed review and instruct rework, decide whether to close/release completed work. - Decision owner: The source-prescribed external orchestrator or responsible human in the consumer project. The Node CLI gathers/checks workboard/current-state evidence and applies selected transitions; it does not autonomously choose among priority alternatives. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WORKBOARD, harness status, harness claim, harness closeout, progress reports, single-active-work gate, review verdicts and CI merge controls. - Closure path: Whole current WORKBOARD and planned clickstop inventory → external manager identifies a current commitment, invokes claim/dispatch → workers return status/review evidence → manager decides correction/reallocation or closeout → first-party CLI updates active work and subsequent assignment context. - Boundary reachability: Consumer installation materializes the workboard and process docs; shipped claim/status/closeout commands read/write actual files and refuse inconsistent transitions. The core Node tool is a constructor for current-control decisions; the source does not auto-launch the orchestrator model. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-coded management acts only when operated by an external agent host; removing that host leaves deterministic workboard plumbing but no discretionary portfolio negotiation. A positive C here identifies a specific delivered S3 control constructor, not automatic S3=A or generic workflow completeness. - Whole-system current view: The active/planned clickstop table and owner/branch/blocked metadata in WORKBOARD plus PR/review state and reporting, at the one consumer delivery-cell recursion. - Current-control decision scope: Active CS allocation/owner, constrained parallelism, failed review return and closeout of a project&#39;s current delivery commitments, not host-company staffing or long-term constitutional goals. - Evidence: claim current-cell view, claim mutation, closeout, orchestrator management role. - Basis: Specific first-party current-control observation, transition and return machinery, with externally owned discretionary S3 right. - Confidence: Medium source-structural. - Caveats: Human/LLM consumer actor is required; no standing self-running S3 agent, whole-system budget negotiator or parent-governed S3 (P) path is established by this source. Workboard alone does not count; the positive constructor depends on its wired claim/rework/closeout loop.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently inspect producer claims against the actual PR head and return findings that alter acceptance/rework decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementer overconfidence, stale review against an old commit, omitted blocking code defects or approval from a reviewer sharing the implementer&#39;s model context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-based reviewer chooses a fresh Go/Needs-Fix and issue findings from reviewed PR evidence, independently of the producing actor; first-party review framework rejects reviewer-model conflicts and refuses to treat blocking findings as approval. - Decision owner: Independently invoked reviewer model (local rubber-duck when supplied, or GitHub Copilot PR reviewer) for factual/technical judgment, with first-party CI acting as enforcement. A model that also implemented the PR is barred by the configured separation rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: assertReviewerAllowed, model audit fields, actual PR HEAD and diff evidence, reviewer prompt, Copilot REST reviewer invocation, per-HEAD review log and managed review-gates.yml required checks. - Closure path: Implementer submits current source/PR → independent model reviews that head and identifies findings → computeVerdict returns No-Go/Needs-Fix on blocking output, logs exact reviewed SHA → review workflow blocks integration until a valid current-head review → orchestrator/worker reworks PR and requests a new round. - Boundary reachability: harness review first-party command genuinely requests Copilot reviewer through GitHub when review config enables it; review-gates.yml is shipped to consumers via init/sync and includes independent/attached evidence checks; source documents the reviewer dispatch as the normal content-PR gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer model assesses producer output from separately obtained actual PR source, chooses critique and can force new work; deterministic log parsing and CI do not themselves own that independent judgment. - Claim being audited: Current implementer/subagent work is correct and ready to merge under its original clickstop acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker completion report and PR author statements, branch status and routine task tests. - Complementary access path: Separate model reviewer analyzes actual PR current head/diff and required reading, with Copilot&#39;s independently attached GitHub review as a distinct channel. - Independence boundary: Different reviewer model from recorded implementers is enforced for local audit; Copilot is a separate GitHub review actor; primary evidence and head SHA are checked instead of recycling author summary alone. - Who acts on findings: First-party verdict and required checks withhold Go/merge evidence; supervised orchestrator triggers repairs and a new review round. - Evidence: review PR and independent-model gate, Copilot review and combined verdict, verdict logic, Copilot actual review, managed enforcement. - Basis: Explicit source-connected complementary reviewer judgment, distinct access and rework/block closure path. - Confidence: Medium-high for configured reviewer-enabled consumer mode; actual real PR review not replayed in this isolated assessment. - Caveats: Without GitHub authentication, enabled review checks and a responding reviewer, the model audit is not executable; source review is not a measured guarantee that Copilot detects all errors. Optional manual-only mode should not be conflated with autonomous reviewer operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Scan external changes with a prospective model, develop options for changing future delivery-cell capability and return chosen adaptation to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in future tooling, supported environments, dependencies or customer delivery needs requiring a changed organizational capability rather than one repaired work item. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped first-party externally prospective adaptation judgment and closed option-to-capability feedback right found. - Decision owner: Not established: harness upgrade compares a user-specified target Git ref deterministically, while harvest lists stale existing learning entries for later operator disposition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Upgrade dry-run diff, version pin and managed sync, LEARNINGS.md harvest, clickstop planning, changelog and maintenance rules. - Closure path: No first-party model of external future conditions that selects among capability change options, returns the prospective decision to S3, and updates later operational capacity without an externally authored plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Upgrade preview and manual version application can change installed files, but the candidate ref and adoption judgment are supplied from outside, not selected through a shipped strategic prospective loop; stale-learning surfacing is retrospective operational improvement. - Evidence: upgrade takes specified ref and previews only, harvest stale items, model/role process configuration. - Basis: Bounded negative at first-party installed/CLI paths, not universal impossibility for custom consumers. - Confidence: Medium; human-managed downstream process evolution can be built from these primitives but is not itself source-closed S4. - Caveats: Consumer humans may perform strategic adaptation; the source does not warrant crediting that unobserved higher-recursion function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve legitimate ultimate-policy/identity disputes over the consumer delivery cell&#39;s enduring mission and reconcile current versus future orientations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting enduring purposes, permissible changes of identity, constitutional operating constraints or ultimate authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established by the first-party shipped process. User approval gates concern task-plan/implementation details or acceptance of a code/review change, not a reconstructable constitutional adjudication path. - Decision owner: No source-connected S5 ultimate-policy actor is evidenced; the adopter or project owner remains external higher recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: INSTRUCTIONS, CODEOFCONDUCT, CONVENTIONS, review rulesets, workboard approval policy, optional owner override, credentials and repository settings. - Closure path: No identity-level issue → legitimate constitutional authority → authoritative decision → re-governed ongoing operations path established in first-party artifact. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Managed policy files and approvals constrain delivery, but do not transform their configured owners into a standing S5 entity. Model roles cannot independently rewrite legitimate identity. - Evidence: operating approval context, review policy, workboard gate. - Basis: Bounded negative following inspection of permission, process governance and approval mechanisms. - Confidence: Medium-high in this assessed recursion. - Caveats: A consumer can attach genuine external S5 institution; it is not first-party authority supplied by the library.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/12eb45991ddd86b008d65053dbcf2dcfbce61ac3/assessments/he/henrik-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 12eb45991ddd86b008d65053dbcf2dcfbce61ac3\">12eb459 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T23:59:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/prasenjeet-symon/ogcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ogcode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/prasenjeet-symon/ogcode/tree/b9077a0fcca60195da4426b3f733933b533be705\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b9077a0fcca60195da4426b3f733933b533be705\">b9077a0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce repository-facing software outcomes through open-ended model/tool coding action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous code structure, incomplete implementation details, syntax/build/test failures, repository state, tool output, external documentation, context limits and changing user requirements encountered during implementation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what project evidence to inspect, which coding/tool action to take, how to respond to returned failures/evidence, what implementation satisfies the assigned outcome and when local work is complete. - Decision owner: model-backed BuildAgent in interactive work and model-backed TaskAgent in each plan worktree. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry; repository maps/search/read/edit/write/bash; syntax checks; provider adapters; permissions; sessions; compaction/memory; worktrees and git; cancellation/timeouts. - Closure path: software goal/task description plus project context → agent selects action/tool → tool/environment result returns into the same agent loop → agent revises implementation or verifies/finishes → repository-facing result is produced. - Boundary reachability: BuildAgent is the normal interactive mode; TaskAgent is constructed by the standard plan/task execution path and runs the shipped coding loop in its worktree without adopter-authored orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed Build/Task actor while retaining tools, worktrees, stores and permission machinery removes the task-specific software-engineering judgment. - Evidence: internal/agent/agent.go; internal/agent/loop.go; internal/server/taskroutes.go; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model inference and GitHub remote service behavior are dependencies; the credited loop/control composition is first-party Ogcode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent destructive interference and merge conflict among parallel implementation S1 workstreams by deciding file ownership/dependency relations before execution and feeding those relations into task scope/order. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently executing TaskAgents can otherwise modify overlapping files from parallel branches, creating incompatible patches/merge conflicts; dependent implementation can also run before prerequisite changes are present. - Distinct S1 units: separate TaskAgent sessions executing different implementation tasks in distinct git worktrees/branches. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping parallel file mutation can produce competing patches at integration time; premature dependent execution can act on a repository state missing predecessor work. - Attenuating coordination relation: BreakdownAgent must assign each file to exactly one parallel workstream and add a dependency whenever two tasks need the same file. It also defines linear dependencies reflecting implementation prerequisites. The runtime then isolates tasks in worktrees and executes dependencies only after predecessor integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: BreakdownAgent&#39;s task descriptions/dependencies become the authoritative TaskAgent prompts and task records; the chosen graph determines whether a task starts concurrently or waits, which branch/worktree it sees, and which files it is instructed to modify. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the first-party breakdown prompt explicitly names the concrete shared-file merge-conflict disturbance and requires file ownership/dependency decisions to attenuate it. Worktrees and dependency scheduling enforce that selected coordination relation rather than merely moving work between agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the task-specific partition of files/workstreams and the dependency relation needed to avoid cross-worker conflict while preserving safe parallelism. - Decision owner: model-backed BreakdownAgent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structured submittaskbreakdown; task dependency records; per-task worktrees/branches; chain branches; dependency readiness checks; atomic task claims; serialized git metadata mutation; automatic dependent startup after predecessor integration. - Closure path: locked plan/repository evidence → BreakdownAgent identifies scopes/dependencies under explicit anti-conflict rules → structured task graph is stored → server creates isolated worktrees and schedules according to that graph → TaskAgents receive scoped descriptions and predecessor state → later S1 operation follows the selected coordination. - Boundary reachability: BreakdownAgent, breakdown tool and plan/task materialization are invoked automatically by the standard locked-plan mode; no consumer-authored coordinator is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing BreakdownAgent leaves deterministic mechanisms capable of enforcing a supplied graph, but removes the discretionary, task-specific choice of which files/workstreams should be parallel versus ordered. - Evidence: internal/agent/breakdown.go; internal/agent/agent.go; internal/tool/breakdown.go; internal/server/taskroutes.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: worktree/dependency enforcement itself is deterministic; autonomy is assigned to the BreakdownAgent&#39;s organizational partition decision, not to git or the scheduler.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: give the legitimate operator a whole-plan current view and authority to intervene in active implementation commitments on behalf of the current software-delivery whole. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a plan may contain pending, blocked, running, failed and completed tasks; a current TaskAgent can stall, take a wrong path, fail, need retry or need operator-directed interruption while other plan commitments continue. - Whole-system current view: the first-party task board groups every task in the active plan by current status, shows total/done/running/failed/pending counts, dependency eligibility and per-task branch/session/PR state. - Current-control decision scope: start one eligible task or all eligible tasks; inspect a running task&#39;s live agent session; abort that running session; mark current work complete/failed where appropriate; retry failed work from a clean task state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which currently eligible commitments are started and when a current task session should be interrupted/retried/failed as part of supervising the active plan. - Decision owner: human/operator at the parent recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan/task stores; task-board projections; task/session routes; server running-loop registry; context cancellation; dependency scheduler; worktree cleanup/retry; SSE/bus status updates. - Closure path: task board/current session exposes whole-plan current state → operator selects start/intervention/retry or abort → first-party API mutates task state or cancels the registered task session loop → task/plan events and dependency eligibility update → subsequent current execution changes. - Boundary reachability: the browser plan/task board and task-execution session are standard product surfaces; TaskAgent loops are registered in the same server running map consumed by the generic first-party session-abort endpoint. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: BreakdownAgent selects the initial coordination graph but does not receive a live whole-plan control loop. Runtime auto-start is deterministic enforcement of the frozen dependency graph. The discretionary current-control mode established in the frozen product belongs to the operator. - Evidence: web/src/pages/plan-tasks.tsx; web/src/pages/task-execution.tsx; internal/server/taskroutes.go; internal/server/sessionroutes.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a parent/operator supervisory mode, not autonomous S3. The deterministic dependency scheduler and automatic completion transitions do not themselves own discretionary whole-system current-control judgment.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit loop was established that independently challenges implementation claims and returns a distinct audit judgment into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation agents can be wrong about the correctness/completeness of their own changes, but the frozen standard distribution does not package a separate reviewer/evaluator role or audit gate with complementary evidence access. - Claim being audited: no standard first-party independent-audit claim/route was established. - Ordinary reporting path: BuildAgent/TaskAgent implement changes and are themselves instructed to run syntax/build/test/lint verification before declaring completion. - Complementary access path: none established as an audit function. The generic task tool can spawn a clean-context read-only investigator over code/web evidence, but its shipped contract is broad investigation/context offload rather than independent review of producer claims, and no standard reviewer role/accept-correct loop is wired around task completion. - Independence boundary: no material audit boundary is established beyond optional generic investigation. - Who acts on findings: no standard independent-audit finding/closure path established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: self-verification in coding prompts; read-only TaskTool investigation; syntax checker; builds/tests; git/PR artifacts. - Closure path: not applicable; no standard complementary auditor judgment is returned as a distinct control input. - Boundary reachability: not applicable for a positive path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary tests and the implementer&#39;s own verification are the production path, while generic clean-context investigation is not packaged as an audit function in the frozen distribution. - Evidence: internal/agent/agent.go; internal/tool/task.go; internal/server/taskroutes.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator or BuildAgent could formulate a review-like prompt for the generic read-only child, but generic subagent expressiveness is not treated as a standard-distribution S3 audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then loop was established that senses external/future change, develops organizational adaptation options and returns a selected adaptation into present capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external documentation/search, prior project decisions, model/tool availability and persistent memory can influence current software work, but none forms a first-party prospective adaptation cycle at the selected organization boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deepsearch and page fetching; PlanAgent; NoteAgent; session/project memory recall; MEMORY.md; skills/MCP/model configuration; compaction; persistent sessions and archived plans. - Closure path: not applicable; no external/future distinction → adaptation-option development → selected persistent capability/strategy change → return to current S3/capability loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: PlanAgent plans current requested software, recall restores past operational context, and search answers present questions. These paths do not establish prospective organizational adaptation merely because they contain research, learning or persistence. - Evidence: README.md; internal/agent/agent.go; internal/tool/memoryrecall.go; internal/tool/projectmemoryrecall.go; internal/agent/memorymd.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the README contains a broader future computer-agent vision; intake explicitly excludes those roadmap claims from current-state evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: carry legitimate project-owner standing instructions/rules into every relevant Ogcode agent context so later software work remains governed by project-level policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding/planning/task agents can otherwise diverge from repository architecture, conventions, risk boundaries or durable owner instructions across sessions/worktrees. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: which durable project rules, priorities and behavioral constraints legitimately govern Ogcode&#39;s work in the selected repository. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Parent (P) — the legitimate project owner/editor owns project AGENTS.md / AGENT.md instruction content. No autonomous agent-owned identity/ultimate-policy mode is established. - Return-to-operation path: Ogcode discovers owner-authored instruction files from root to current project directory, preserves root-to-leaf precedence, loads their text into the agent system-prompt construction and thereby governs later Build/Plan/Task execution in that project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the durable project-level instructions and precedence that should constrain future agent operation. - Decision owner: human/project owner at the parent recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: instruction-file discovery/loading; root-to-leaf ordering; prompt block rendering/escaping; system prompt builder; ordinary file/version-control persistence. - Closure path: project-level policy issue → legitimate owner changes AGENTS.md/AGENT.md → Ogcode reloads project instructions for agent execution → instructions enter system context → subsequent software operation is governed by the returned policy. - Boundary reachability: AGENTS.md/AGENT.md loading is first-party runtime code invoked by the standard agent prompt construction; it is not a contributor-only plugin or external wrapper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents consume the standing instructions but do not receive a first-party mechanism making their self-authored policy legitimate ultimate authority; ownership remains with the project authority that supplies the files. - Evidence: internal/agent/agentmd.go; internal/agent/loop.go; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permission prompts and ordinary task/plan approval are operational controls and are not used as S5 evidence; S5 is limited to durable project-recursion policy returned through the project instruction surface.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/84c26bb06c83f644c72a156d8214cfcb5a19bcf8/assessments/ogcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 84c26bb06c83f644c72a156d8214cfcb5a19bcf8\">84c26bb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:12:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/proliferate-ai/proliferate\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Proliferate</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-16\">2026-09-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/proliferate-ai/proliferate/tree/74e1178cf3ecfbfc3ffeb8321f82c4a9bfce620f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 74e1178cf3ecfbfc3ffeb8321f82c4a9bfce620f\">74e1178 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: standard product sessions run native coding-agent harnesses that autonomously inspect, edit and execute work inside bounded task workspaces; child sessions can independently complete delegated scoped work and return results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: first-party isolation, parent→child messaging and exact completion wakeups are conflict avoidance/delegation mechanisms. The pinned implementation does not provide an agent-owned mutual-adjustment loop that observes and dampens interference or oscillation among peer S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the product control plane provisions workspaces, sessions and workflows, while a parent may supervise its own delegated children, but no distinct autonomous actor is given current-whole authority to regulate shared resources, commitments or priorities across the operational system. Scheduling/provisioning alone is not Beer S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the first-party review runtime establishes an independent audit path. Review-only agents inspect trusted plan/code targets, are prohibited from modifying them, submit structured pass/critique results, and return findings to the parent session. The runtime can automatically iterate parent revision → trusted recapture → fresh review round until pass or a configured round limit. This is a ready, agent-enacted complementary audit/feedback loop rather than a developer-supplied verifier primitive.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: recurring/event-driven workflows react to configured events and can run tasks such as triage or dependency updates, but the pinned distribution does not establish a persistent agent-owned external/prospective intelligence function that models environmental change and feeds adaptation into present control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: harness choice, workflow definitions, reviewer configuration, permissions and review policy remain product/operator configuration. No first-party agent holds final authority over organizational identity, purpose or constitutional policy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ff74c380425fb7c8a9cee512289cdb428d74dbb1/assessments/proliferate.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ff74c380425fb7c8a9cee512289cdb428d74dbb1\">ff74c38 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T04:20:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Darwin-Agent/HarnessX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HarnessX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Darwin-Agent/HarnessX/tree/915520584c92cc35ed24aef1af17eec1d60c0b01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 915520584c92cc35ed24aef1af17eec1d60c0b01\">9155205 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Conduct current user/evaluation task work by selecting available tools through the task agent and adapting next model actions after real tool execution observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New task/environment inputs, failing or successful real tools, edits/observations, unexpected model answers, session interruptions and capability budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured model produces tool calls and subsequent decisions; source-owned RunLoop executes registered tool callbacks and inserts real results into task state/conversation; this changes next possible actions and the produced task result. - Decision owner: First-party HarnessX installed model-backed task agent using third-party inference through an explicit provider boundary. Tool registry/hooks and model budgets limit discretionary task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HarnessConfig, RunLoop, tool registry and ToolResultEvent, state/trajectory, hook processors, workspace and sandbox, cost guard and interruption/resume. - Closure path: Task → Harness.run → model-driven RunLoop → selected registered tool call → executable handler → ToolResultEvent appended to state → next model turn/action/final deliverable → task trajectory, used by evolution. - Boundary reachability: Shipped public Harness.run and provider configuration lead directly to runloop with actual tool registry execution and observation persistence. This is source-level production code, not just an interface or benchmark score. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool choice and subsequent result-informed task continuation belong to the configured model-backed agent; process hooks and static restrictions are safeguards and do not stand in for autonomous task decisions. - Evidence: harnessx/core/harness.py; harnessx/core/harness.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py. - Basis: First-party model/tool/result closure in shipped operational runtime. - Confidence: High structural confidence; no external provider inference executed. - Caveats: Deterministic processor execution without a configured model is not by itself S1=A. External benchmark correctness is unverified.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party disturbance-specific reciprocal coordination role across independently viable S1 operating units is established at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel evaluation tasks or agent subprocesses may share budgets, files and outputs; runtime processors and meta-agent write-scope gates constrain local conflicts rather than observing competing viable units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Tool call/processor ordering, task timeout, separate round/config workspaces, and write-path gates block or sequence current work. There is no shipped S2 arbitration decision choosing mutual adjustments between independent S1 units from sensed interference. - Decision owner: Per-task RunLoop and static plugin/recipe configuration, external operator for parallelism; no legitimate standing S2 controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Processor pipeline, workspace/sandbox isolation, write/read scopes, run/round files and configured evaluation concurrency. - Closure path: Tool tasks/rounds → isolated/sequenced execution → results; missing identified inter-S1 operational disturbance → agent-selected damping policy → returned reciprocal operating restrictions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker scheduling, output isolation and sandbox hardening are infrastructure, not an evidenced VSM S2-specific inter-unit interference regulator. - Evidence: harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/metaharness/processors/writescopegate.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; harnessx/core/processor.py. - Basis: Bounded absence after testing parallel tasks, hooks, meta-agent workspace/write conflict surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high for frozen first-party sample modes. - Caveats: A multi-tenant adopter could use plugins to implement such policies; hypothetical external implementations do not count.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No owner makes binding whole-current priority/resource/commitment choices over separate ongoing viable S1 divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed evaluations, incomplete rounds, step/cost limits and rejected harness edits affect current experiment progress; no multi-division current operating commitments are modeled. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Recipe controller selects fixed benchmark task sets and configured round order; meta-agent proposes harness redesign, the validator rejects invalid candidates, and acceptance applies to future model-agent configs. There is no first-party global portfolio operating priority decision with S1 management intervention. - Decision owner: External experiment operator and code-configured round runner; local agent and meta-agent for task/design choices, not an accountable whole-current S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Round state, currentconfig, task eval logs, budgets, postflight validation, recovery/fallback for the experiment. - Closure path: Configured run → eval and improvement → next round; missing whole-current multi-unit status → legitimate obligations/resource tradeoff decision → binding cross-unit current action return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Meta-agent evolves capability across rounds (S4 here), rather than holding a standing current-control mandate for multiple separate viable S1 operating cells. - Evidence: recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/gaiaevolver/run.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py. - Basis: Tested meta-agent, benchmark controller and task manager candidates against S3 scope. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Human research team/resource management or downstream customer program control are not in the first-party running harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently sourced complementary audit of a standing S3&#39;s operational claims with returned corrective management authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfit candidate configs, invalid contracts, failing replay and incorrect task results are detectable; evidence quality and safety are safeguarded but not used to audit whole-current management reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: EvolveValidator checks canonicalization, contract, replay and policy and may reject a proposed evolved config. Benchmark evaluator/LLM judge may score task output; a rejection returns to next harness edit or run fallback, not to any legitimate S3 owner required to correct current organizational commitments. - Decision owner: Validation and replay implementation plus externally configured benchmark grader; no separate organizational audit with independent direct operating-fact access and governing S3 corrective return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Validation report, changeset, replay smoke check, novelty/evidence gate, cross-round journal and per-task scoring. - Closure path: Proposed config → validity/replay/policy check → permit or reject for next experiment. Missing independent observation of S3 current-performance claim → challenge → binding S3 remedy applied across S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate technical validation gate can be effective without being S3: source shows config/test validity and task scoring, not a complementary management reality-check function with its own return. - Evidence: harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/replay.py. - Basis: First-party strongest audit-candidate source path inspected and bounded to config/task checking. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External independent benchmarks are not automatically first-party independent organizational auditor; their scoring policy cannot be borrowed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Use encountered external task/evaluation outcomes to autonomously redesign future agent-harness capabilities, validate new viable options and commit an accepted changed configuration for later operating rounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Past harness may fail unknown future tasks; patterns in new environments and actual trajectories reveal deficiencies in instructions, processors, tools, and resource/safety boundaries that motivate future changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model-backed MetaAgent diagnoses prior task trajectories and autonomously writes candidate changed HarnessConfig plus prompt/processor implementations. EvolveValidator enforces source-owned validity/contract/replay/novelty gates; the shipped multi-round recipe promotes a valid candidate into durable round config and sets it as currentconfig for the next evaluator/task-agent execution. - Decision owner: Configured MetaAgent model for proposed future capability and technical validation controller for admission; the first-party executable evolver recipe is the accepted-change authority within the explicitly bounded experiment. No claim of wider business approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Trajectory collection and benchmark feedback, journal of hypotheses/round history, model-backed config editor, write scope and budget, canonicalization/contract/replay checks, output config plus promoted round state/resume file. - Closure path: External evaluation tasks and failures → task trajectories and cross-round context → MetaAgent model diagnoses/generates alternative future prompt/tools/processors → EvolveValidator checks new config → recipe promoteroundoutput and updates persisted lastoutputconfig / currentconfig → next round uses changed executable HarnessConfig and creates fresh feedback. - Boundary reachability: recipe/tb2evolver/run.py is a first-party executable: MetaAgent(...) constructed at line 635, each iteration reads current trajectories then calls metaagent.evolve at line 710, adapter promoteroundoutput at 718, state persists lastoutputconfig at 746, and currentconfig=promotedconfig at 751. Separate GAIA recipe writes currentconfig=candidatecfg after validation; no optional custom external adopter glue is needed. - External distinction: Task benchmark/environment outcomes are observed from separately executed tasks and actual candidate trajectories, not inferred from static configuration alone. They include failures and task variation across rounds; benchmark grader policy and provider internal operations stay outside HarnessX ownership. - Future / prospective distinction: The MetaAgent modifies reusable prompt/processor/tool harness configuration for following, distinct evaluation tasks and rounds, rather than merely choosing the best answer to the present user&#39;s one request. Round state and journal deliberately carry changed capability forward. - Adaptation option generated: A model-authorized configuration editor proposes new prompt templates, hook processors, configured tool paths and other allowed harness levers from observed failure patterns. Novelty and replay checks distinguish viable alternatives from a repeated/no-op or invalid modification. - Path back into current capability / S3: After successful source-owned postflight, the recipe promotes the new config path, records it in persisted run state and loads that same config as currentconfig for subsequent ordinary Harness.run task evaluation. This closes directly into the next S1 operating capability at the harness-improvement recursion; no independent organization-wide S3 is assumed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model selection of what harness should become, based on external task evidence, combines with first-party validation and actual promotion into later execution. This is a concrete prospective, executable capability loop and not only a memory/summary or evaluation score. - Evidence: harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/journal.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/gaiaevolver/run.py; recipe/gaiaevolver/run.py. - Basis: Independent first-party S4 option/change/admission and installed next-round loop; positive limited to configured, source-wired harness-improvement experiment. - Confidence: Medium-high for source reachability and capability adoption, not benchmark effectiveness. - Caveats: The validator can reject proposed configs and produce no new capability; not every iteration improves scores. Promotion applies to experimental harness config rather than a managed external production service or approved enterprise deployment. User supplies experiment scope and models. First-party recipe may deliberately accept a valid but not empirically improved config; S4=A here indicates a future-capability adaptation mechanism, not guaranteed selection of a better policy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No system-owned ultimate legitimate purpose/identity policy arbiter adjudicates what goals the harness-improvement organization must pursue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Allowed tool/build paths, model cost, replay bounds and evidence rules constrain experiments but do not legitimate a changed institutional purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human selects benchmark/task pool/provider, allowed write roots and budgets; EvolveValidator and gates permit/reject one future config according to those preset criteria. No S5 authority changes the ultimate ethical/purpose contract across the system. - Decision owner: External adopter/research experiment operator for goals, data rights and provider choices; first-party validation engines for local code safety, not final governing mission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Meta-agent write scope, run cost/timeout, canonical config schema, validation/novelty policy, benchmark setup and repo maintenance/license. - Closure path: Preset experiment objective → candidate design/replay → future config. Missing conflicting ultimate values/identity interpretations → legitimate final authority decision → enforceable mission/purpose return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source technical acceptance of one capability modification (S4) is narrower than authorizing the whole organization&#39;s ultimate identity and purpose (S5). - Evidence: harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/processors/writescopegate.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py. - Basis: First-party config-policy, safety and external institutional-right boundary inspected. - Confidence: Medium-high at declared recursion. - Caveats: The external university/company/deployer may own legal purpose; this package does not instantiate such a VSM owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4143efce3ef5f626dc3211866f7cd1409773ec6d/assessments/harnessx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4143efce3ef5f626dc3211866f7cd1409773ec6d\">4143efc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T18:03:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/garudust-org/garudust-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Garudust Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/garudust-org/garudust-agent/tree/905e7797fc1f71782c88e3af0ff7dfb7b9aca61a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 905e7797fc1f71782c88e3af0ff7dfb7b9aca61a\">905e779 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform human-scoped tool-bearing tasks and deliver answers or artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable user, workspace, web and tool outcomes, provider feedback and execution errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select/revise tool calls, use returned results, continue or complete. - Decision owner: Principal model-driven Agent and model-driven child instances for delegated tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider transport, typed ToolRegistry, iteration budget, guarded execution groups, local memory and prompt builder. - Closure path: Current task -&gt; LLM/tool decision -&gt; guarded tool call -&gt; tool result -&gt; LLM chooses another call, delegation, or answer. - Boundary reachability: Agent::run drives public terminal, server and desktop paths; DelegateTask calls SubAgentRunner::runtask in the same shipped crate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model owns substantive task actions; provider backend and registry execute/guard them. - Evidence: Agent implementation, Tool trait, README operational modes. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No qualifying inter-S1 adaptive coordination was evidenced despite optional parallel child execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple child agents could choose incompatible edits or outputs on a shared workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Parent instructs children to take independent tasks; fixed fan-out limit, result ordering and intra-call conflict serialization do not themselves resolve cross-child conflicts, and no separate cross-child coordinator was found. - Decision owner: No qualifying function-specific first-party coordination owner; the parent may reconcile outcomes as a normal whole-task S3 step. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DelegateTasks concurrency cap, child output indexing and failure marking; parallelismkey serializes same-agent tool calls with conflicting keys. - Closure path: Independent delegated tasks are launched -&gt; results returned in order -&gt; parent can retry/accept; no evidenced separate shared inter-child interference regulation returns a coordinated joint operating rule. - Boundary reachability: Delegate tools are shipped, but none of the code examined proves shared child workspace lock/scoped-write arbitration, team task claims, or inter-child feedback logic. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic parallelism and parent-level post-hoc integration do not make S2; a key conflict guard regulates concurrent tool invocations, not autonomous child relations. - Distinct S1 units: Model-operated child runs that may execute at once. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Potential overlapping shared workspace mutation or incompatible outputs. - Attenuating coordination relation: Not established as a distinct inter-S1 coordination mechanism; only request that tasks be independent and fixed concurrency cap. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Failed child outputs return to parent for whole-task replan (S3), not a verified inter-child coordination channel. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: No source showed that additional S2-specific intervention; absence is intentional, not a claim that the system cannot ever be configured to coordinate. - Evidence: delegation, concurrency key trait, agent grouped dispatch. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate a whole present task&#39;s plan, subgoal division, unsuccessful work and final acceptance across optional parallel subagents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncompleted parts, failed delegated tasks, budget pressure, inconsistent outputs and user goal continuation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Parent model decomposes current goal, chooses independent subtasks, consumes indexed failures/successes and chooses retry/integration/answer within the bounded task. - Decision owner: Parent Agent model; not DelegateTasks cap or persistence code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalStore per conversation, iteration budget, delegation tool results, memory and status messages in model history. - Closure path: Current goal and work -&gt; model chooses delegated task set -&gt; returned partial outcomes and failures are visible -&gt; parent adjusts plan or verifies deliverable -&gt; follow-up S1 calls or final answer. - Boundary reachability: Public delegatetasks tool and native Agent::run loop; behavior explicitly documented in tool description and output shaping. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses current subtask scope and response; the Rust budget, child ordering and session persistence merely support its discretion. - Whole-system current view: Goal + model history with child statuses/outputs for one user&#39;s bounded task. - Current-control decision scope: Continue/retry/accept present task subtasks, not governance of multiple independently owned deployments. - Evidence: delegate tasks API, Agent runner, GoalStore. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped, distinct complementary evidence-challenge audit path was established for running customer task outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model-written work can be confidently wrong without a separately sourced falsification channel. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Generic child delegation can be prompted to review output, but no first-party reviewer role/trigger, raw-source independent access agreement and receipt-to-parent feedback path is supplied as a standard construction. - Decision owner: Application/user might designate an independent reviewer; no qualifying shipped auditor owner observed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: General child tools, regular tool/response messages, development PR review CI and tests. - Closure path: Ordinary tool return is not an independent audit. Repository Claude PR review inspects Garudust source contributions, not each end-user run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A possible custom reviewer prompt is external composition, and generic self-critique or a failed tool is not S3. - Claim being audited: Customer-task deliverable correctness. - Ordinary reporting path: Principal model reports its work and child completions. - Complementary access path: No first-party separately tasked challenger with raw-evidence and returned verdict established. - Independence boundary: Not demonstrated. - Who acts on findings: User or custom orchestrator if added; none guaranteed. - Evidence: general child delegation, agent loop, repository PR-review CI excluded. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No sourced future-oriented external variety sensing -&gt; capability option selection -&gt; present governance return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future market, tool, model and environmental change beyond the current user request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model can search the web, learn preferences and create reusable skills from completed work, but the shipped process is reactive to current tasks/experience. - Decision owner: No distinct first-party prospective intelligence owner; human can install tools and skills. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: websearch, skillview/local skills, memory, scheduled task creation, user install commands. - Closure path: Tool results or local lessons support current/future tool use, but without a demonstrable external-and-then prospective strategy feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Saving a learned skill, extracting a preference and running cron jobs are not by themselves S4. - Evidence: skill loader, prompt instructions, cron tools, README. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ongoing legitimate ultimate-identity/purpose conflict resolution and returned policy commitment found in the assessed personal-task agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Contradictory foundational purposes, user persona requirements and authority conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human owner can edit SOUL.md, profile, config and tool-approval modes; these are external inputs or tactical tool permissions, not an evidenced S5 governance decision loop. - Decision owner: Legitimate user/operator outside the runtime. No first-party S5-specific closing controller established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: System prompt from static agent identity + operator SOUL.md, user profile and AGENTS.md, host CLI config. - Closure path: Operator-provided identity text is injected at run start; no source proves a first-party identity-issue -&gt; deliberate human acceptance/rejection -&gt; enduring policy update pathway analogous to a governed mandate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompt rules and generic destructive-action approval constrain S1 without permitting the agent to own or settle its ultimate purpose. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Durable foundational persona/mission, beyond episodic answer or user preference memory. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No positive first-party S5 mode asserted; user owns static configuration. - Return-to-operation path: Static SOUL.md and AGENTS.md load into prompt but are not a complete S5 governance mechanism. - Evidence: prompt builder, configuration, approval policies. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8333415f55a9fe165d06642345bea68a9d80d4a6/assessments/ga/garudust-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8333415f55a9fe165d06642345bea68a9d80d4a6\">8333415 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T22:25:19Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Growth-Circle/cadis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">C.A.D.I.S.</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Growth-Circle/cadis/tree/52c55854b1abbcda82839b7a934cbdb16a69b635\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 52c55854b1abbcda82839b7a934cbdb16a69b635\">52c5585 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user objectives through a model-driven tool/action loop that receives environment/tool observations and chooses subsequent operational action until completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: request ambiguity, workspace/file/git/shell state, tool results, provider responses, failed actions and intermediate evidence that changes the next useful operational step. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which available tool/action to invoke, its arguments, whether further action is needed and the final operational response within configured safety/step bounds. - Decision owner: the model-driven CADIS agent running through the daemon&#39;s first-party generation/tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, parsetoolcalldirectives, native tool registry, workspace grants, policy engine, approvals, session/step limits, persistence and daemon event streaming. - Closure path: request enters cadisd → routed agent/model generates a tool directive or response → daemon executes the selected tool under policy/workspace controls → tool results are placed into a follow-up prompt → model chooses the next action or completes → result returns to the caller/environment. - Boundary reachability: ordinary message.send through the shipped daemon executes this first-party loop; no downstream organizational actor must be supplied to obtain model/action/feedback closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy, grants and bounds can reject or constrain choices, but they do not predetermine the substantive tool/action sequence. Removing the model actor removes the ordinary operational decision path. - Evidence: crates/cadis-daemon/src/main.rs; crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: direct client commands and deterministic policy/approval branches also exist. A is based on the standard model-owned tool/action path, not on every request transition being autonomous.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among distinct concurrently active CADIS operational agents/workers by isolating project mutations and bounding shared worker concurrency. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel coding units can corrupt or overwrite the same working tree, while unbounded fan-out can consume shared daemon capacity and delay peer work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/revise the cross-S1 isolation and shared-concurrency regime that determines where a coding worker may mutate files and whether another worker may begin now or must wait. - Decision owner: no autonomous first-party S2 actor owns that regime. The standard daemon supplies the function-specific worktree/queue relation and deterministically enforces parent/configured limits, leaving revision of the regime outside autonomous S2 ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-worker worktree intent and metadata, Git worktree creation, workspace/grant checks, worktree-local worker command cwd, maxconcurrentworkers, global running-worker count, queued worker state and slot-release/start logic. - Closure path: multiple S1 tasks enter parallel worker execution → daemon allocates project work into separate owned worktrees and counts current running workers → excess workers remain Queued and isolated workers operate on separate branches/paths → freed slots start waiting workers and their subsequent execution proceeds under the isolated context. - Boundary reachability: worktree-backed worker operation and global worker admission are shipped daemon/core paths at the pinned revision; callers do not need to implement their own isolation or worker queue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the disturbance and attenuation relation are first-party and specific to cross-unit interference, but isolation/admission outcomes are mechanically derived from configured policy rather than selected by an autonomous coordinating model. This supports C, not A. - Evidence: crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; docs/10RISKREGISTER.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic @agent routing and child-agent spawning are not credited as S2 by themselves. Positive credit rests on the documented parallel-edit/resource disturbances and the first-party isolation/backpressure relation. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately routed/spawned model-driven agents/agent sessions executing operational tasks inside the same cadisd, including coding tasks represented by daemon-managed worker records. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous project mutations can conflict on the same repository state, and concurrent worker fan-out can exceed shared worker execution capacity even when each task is locally valid. - Attenuating coordination relation: CADIS assigns project workers separate Git worktrees and applies a daemon-wide maximum number of running workers, holding excess work in Queued state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the owned worktree becomes the worker&#39;s execution context/cwd for validation and artifacts, while queued workers do not begin until a shared slot is released; both relations change where or when each S1&#39;s next operation occurs. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited mechanisms are explicitly aimed at cross-agent file-conflict and aggregate fan-out disturbances, not merely at transporting messages, naming specialists or ordering a task graph.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain a current organization-wide view of agent commitments and use it to allocate/change present operational work, with an additional legitimate parent/operator intervention mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicated or poorly allocated agent work, changing agent/session status, current task commitments, runaway/unwanted current activity and aggregate worker pressure can make the daemon&#39;s present operation incoherent or exceed current capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomous mode — decide from current all-agent state whether to create a new child agent and assign it a task; parent mode — inspect the current roster and terminate an agent or cancel a session, removing associated current commitments/workers from later operation. - Decision owner: autonomous mode — the main CADIS model actor receiving first-party current runtime state; parent mode — the legitimate self-hosting operator using shipped CLI/HUD/protocol controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: orchestratorawarenesscontext(), agent/session registries and sorted snapshots, recent-session task/step summaries, bounded model-driven [SPAWN] parsing, spawn limits, global worker scheduler, agent.list, agent.kill, session.cancel, persistence and cancellation propagation. - Closure path: current agent roster/session state is assembled → injected into the main agent&#39;s prompt → main model selects a new child role/task when current organization needs it → runtime creates the child and task-bearing session → subsequent organization state includes the changed commitment. In parent mode, roster inspection → kill/cancel request → agent/session and non-terminal associated worker cancellation → subsequent live operation reflects the intervention. - Boundary reachability: whole-agent awareness and model-driven child spawning are standard first-party runtime paths; parent list/kill/cancel operations are stable shipped protocol/CLI/HUD surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main actor receives whole-organization current state rather than only one delegated task and autonomously changes current commitments. Deterministic spawn/worker limits constrain that authority but do not choose the child task. The parent path is separately owned by the operator, producing the (P) modifier. - Evidence: crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; docs/29PROTOCOLFREEZE.md; crates/cadis-cli/src/main.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: ordinary delegation is not credited as S3 by itself. The positive autonomous mapping depends specifically on the main agent&#39;s injected whole-roster/recent-session state plus its live model-owned commitment-creation path. Worker concurrency limits are supporting current-control enforcement, not the reason for A. - Whole-system current view: orchestratorawarenesscontext() enumerates the current agent roster with status, specialist and latest task and also includes recent agent sessions with state and step/budget progress; this generated state is injected into the main orchestrator prompt. - Current-control decision scope: autonomous creation/allocation of new child-agent commitments in response to current organization state, plus parent termination/cancellation of existing live commitments; daemon worker admission additionally constrains concurrent execution.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit path is established for the declared CADIS operating boundary at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional reviewer/tester specialist roles, worker validation commands/test-report artifacts, patch-apply preflight, policy checks, approvals, logs and lifecycle events. - Closure path: not established as S3. These paths validate ordinary outputs or expose ordinary evidence but do not establish a materially independent complementary challenge of an S1/S3 operational claim with a required corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a role named Reviewer can be spawned/routed, but it uses the common agent contract and is not packaged as a mandatory complementary audit owner with distinct evidence access/authority. Deterministic validation/preflight is routine production QA/integrity control. - Evidence: docs/standards/10AGENTSTANDARD.md; crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; docs/29PROTOCOLFREEZE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: a downstream organization can deliberately instantiate a reviewer with different workspace/test access. That would be a separate system-in-focus and does not upgrade the standard CADIS distribution here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then adaptation loop is established for the declared CADIS operating boundary at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as prospective adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent memory records/capsules, workspace/project facts, correction/procedure memory kinds, provider/model selection, specialist personas, configuration reload and project/workspace state. - Closure path: no first-party operating path was found that distinguishes an external/future capability issue, generates an adaptation option, selects it and returns a changed capability or S3 regime into subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: cadis-memory can store candidate records, explicitly promote/reject them and inject already-confirmed capsules, but cadis-core only consumes confirmed memory. The standard runtime does not wire autonomous evidence-to-adaptation proposal/selection/promotion closure. - Evidence: crates/cadis-memory/src/lib.rs; crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is established for the declared CADIS operating boundary at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at identity/ultimate-policy level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no legitimate first-party ultimate authority is wired to adjudicate or amend the organization&#39;s governing identity/policy when such an issue arises. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hardcoded/default CADIS identity prompt, specialist persona fields and agent.specialist.set, central policy engine, workspace grants, risk classes, approval state and ordinary model/provider configuration. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → adjudication/amendment → subsequent-operation-under-new-policy chain is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the named CADIS persona and parent-editable specialist persona configure operating roles; policy/approval components enforce already-selected constraints. Neither surface establishes ultimate-policy ownership merely because it changes prompts or blocks risky actions. - Evidence: crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; docs/29PROTOCOLFREEZE.md; crates/cadis-policy/src/lib.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence - Confidence: high - Caveats: agent.specialist.set and parent configuration can materially change behavior, but task/role/persona configuration is not automatically the organization&#39;s identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/76cc1d6c195ab75cb4dea51f540dd3d685cf4700/assessments/cadis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 76cc1d6c195ab75cb4dea51f540dd3d685cf4700\">76cc1d6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-07T15:23:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hikaMaeng/ndx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ndx</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-07\">2026-10-07</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hikaMaeng/ndx/tree/b1cb16e9b3b3fcd514c9b71a199e10490b7900d7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b1cb16e9b3b3fcd514c9b71a199e10490b7900d7\">b1cb16e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding work by interpreting a user request, selecting first-party tools, inspecting/modifying the workspace, running commands or sandboxed tools, and iterating from tool results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, codebase state, tool/process results, errors, provider responses and evolving user/session context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next operational tool/action, interpret returned evidence, revise the approach, optionally delegate bounded work, and decide when the answer/work is complete. - Decision owner: the ndx root model-backed coding agent; child runAgent loops can own bounded delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent loop, tool registry/worker processes, Docker sandbox for eligible paths, session/runtime persistence, streaming events, max-turn guard, project/user context loading. - Closure path: user input → model decision → first-party tool action → tool result/history → next model decision → further action or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: runAgent is directly wired into the product runtime/session server and creates the first-party tool registry at the frozen revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime executes, persists and constrains operations, but the task-specific choice of tool, interpretation of results and next action comes from the model loop. - Evidence: loop.ts, tool-execution.ts, architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference is external; sandbox/configuration and human environment bound reachable actions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently evidenced first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple child agents can share one cwd and therefore can in principle interfere through overlapping files/dependencies, but the reviewed ndx path does not establish a concrete first-party attenuation policy for that disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decision right is established beyond generic delegation, messaging/waiting and per-child lifecycle operations. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent, sendinput/sendmessage, waitagent, per-record state, CSV worker creation, generic tool-result feedback. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can create well-scoped children and wait/message them, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat delegation, messaging, shared state or sequencing alone as S2. The TypeScript controller supplies no disjoint worktree/file-ownership assignment, collision detector, dependency frontier, mutual-exclusion policy or repair/feedback loop tied to inter-S1 interference. - Evidence: agents.ts, subagents.ts, agent-loop-analysis.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: spawnagentsoncsv declares concurrency-related arguments at the tool-schema layer, but the reviewed TypeScript implementation iterates rows and calls spawnAgent without implementing an S2-specific interference controller.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: observe and regulate the live set of subordinate child-agent commitments during the current coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: running/completed/failed/closed child work, children that should be waited on or terminated, the need to launch additional bounded work, and settled children that need a subsequent turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect child lifecycle snapshots, decide to spawn additional children, wait on selected children, close/abort a selected active child, and start a subsequent turn for a settled child. - Decision owner: the root ndx model-backed agent through model-callable collaboration tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared SubAgentController, stable child ids, per-agent status/error/finalText timestamps, AbortController, child promises, listagents, waitagent, closeagent, resume/follow-up/input tools. - Closure path: root requests current agent-set/status evidence → model decides a current intervention → controller applies spawn/wait/close/resume/follow-up action → updated lifecycle/result evidence returns as a tool result → root decides subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: the collaboration tools are registered in the first-party tool registry and dispatch through ToolContext.agentController; both root runtime and nested child runs receive the shared controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the TypeScript controller stores status and executes lifecycle operations, while the model chooses which child to observe, wait on, stop, resume or task next. Removing the model leaves lifecycle machinery without the same task-specific current-control judgment. - Whole-system current view: listagents returns snapshots for the controller&#39;s known agent set, including status, task prompt, final text/error and timestamps; waitagent returns selected current/final snapshots after a bounded wait. - Current-control decision scope: child spawn, selective wait, targeted close/abort, resume/reuse of a closed child record, and follow-up/input into a non-running child. The reviewed controller does not provide Rust Codex&#39;s full mailbox/watch graph, so the S3 claim is bounded to this implemented child set. - Evidence: subagents.ts, agents.ts, agent-loop-analysis.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: sendinput on an already-running child returns a queued status in the reviewed implementation without a Rust-style durable mailbox; S3 is therefore credited from the implemented view/wait/close/spawn/reuse control surface, not from unsupported live-message semantics.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent first-party audit/reviewer loop with corrective closure is established inside the shipped ndx runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S3 independence/closure. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic child-agent creation, model-pool field named reviewer, repository-development .codex/skills/code-review, builtin command metadata named review, ordinary tests/tool results. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming/configuration surfaces do not instantiate a distinct reviewer owner. The reviewed TypeScript subagent path records agenttype metadata but launches the same runAgent config/client/tool environment; no first-party reviewer restriction, fresh evidence contract or returned veto/corrective gate is wired. - Evidence: subagents.ts, config/index.ts, session command registry. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository-maintainer code-review skills may help contributors review ndx itself, but they are excluded from the installed coding-agent organizational boundary and cannot donate S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external-and-prospective capability-adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md/skill discovery, provider/model settings, session history/logs, environment context and manual configuration. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ndx can load instructions/skills and users can alter configuration, but no reviewed first-party model loop autonomously identifies future/environmental change, generates an adaptation option and writes/promotes it into continuing product capability. - Evidence: initial-context/skill architecture described in agent-loop-analysis.md, architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reloadable project/user resources are operator-authored extensibility, not autonomous S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured instructions, permission/sandbox settings, project/user AGENTS.md, tool/MCP configuration and local user authority. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these mechanisms constrain or configure current operation; they do not grant a model-backed actor legitimate authority to decide ndx&#39;s ultimate identity/purpose/policy and return that decision as binding governance. - Evidence: config/index.ts, architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user/developer configuration is an external authority boundary, not by itself a parent-governed S5 loop under Methodology 0.3.6.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b9fcf4c55f11bb9f24139040fce58f2bd1bea53/assessments/ndx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b9fcf4c55f11bb9f24139040fce58f2bd1bea53\">9b9fcf4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-17T21:24:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ivankuznetsov/hive\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hive (workflow engine)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-17\">2026-11-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ivankuznetsov/hive/tree/9229e25af42398979a8b11ff923e98471f55f543\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9229e25af42398979a8b11ff923e98471f55f543\">9229e25 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute an authored task via model-backed stage agents and durable artifacts - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain requirements, implementation choices and tool results - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose research, planning, code edits and task-specific responses within an admitted stage - Decision owner: External model-backed agent CLI spawned by Hive as a supported workflow stage - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ruby stage runner, CLI profiles, tracked stage markers, state and code worktrees - Closure path: Stage agent writes artifact/commit; daemon checks marker and advances or returns for correction - Boundary reachability: Installed coding/content workflows invoke actual configured Claude/Codex/Pi/Grok/other supported CLI processes - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent decides task content; Hive&#39;s deterministic machinery transports and validates results, rather than originating a code plan - Evidence: docs/concepts.md; docs/architecture.md; README.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate interference between dependent task worktrees and their shared project branch - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent task branches may rely on unfinished prerequisites or incompatible Git bases - Decisive decision or feedback right: Apply dependency gate, reject cycles, select stacked or unstacked branch base, and withhold an ineligible task - Decision owner: First-party deterministic task dependency admission with operator-authored dependency declarations - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stage-gated dependson edges, cycle detection, worktree pointers, stable task metadata - Closure path: Unmet dependency blocks admission until prerequisite stage and branch evidence change; later work inherits the valid base - Boundary reachability: Packaged workflows and CLI/daemon resolve the dependency graph for real project tasks - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This S2-specific coordination path exists but has no agent-owned discretionary resolution; autonomous choice requires composition - Distinct S1 units: Separate coding-agent tasks with isolated feature worktrees within a shared repository - Inter-S1 disturbance: Dependent work may use a stale or incompatible base before a prerequisite completes - Attenuating coordination relation: Dependency edge and branch stacking/fan-in policy validated at scheduling and execution - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Stage transition releases dependency gate and selects correct future task base - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The protocol checks real inter-task branch dependencies and cycle/integration hazards, beyond simply passing messages or order - Evidence: wiki/decisions.md (ADR-056); docs/concepts.md; docs/architecture.md - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Current portfolio control of project tasks, blockers, retries and recoverable workflow commitments - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider limits, errors, waiting questions and stale CI can block multiple ongoing tasks - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose what to retry, answer, hold, approve or grant using a fresh operational-action observation - Decision owner: Legitimate local project operator using TUI, native web, bot or CLI - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Durable operational status/action descriptors, journal, leases, daemon recovery and error markers - Closure path: Operator command changes task state/permission so the daemon can dispatch or recover subsequent work - Boundary reachability: Shipped hive status --operational, hive act, review-grant and TUI all expose this live path - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The daemon computes readiness but does not own the substantive owner-granted decision to escalate or resume blocked work - Whole-system current view: Registered project task queue, running stages, blockers, scheduler freshness and action descriptors - Current-control decision scope: Live task dispatch, recovery authorization, review grants and human escalation at one project recursion - Evidence: lib/hive/operationalstatus.rb; wiki/stages/review.md; README.md - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently assess implementation changes from direct code/CI evidence and return corrective findings - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementation agent can produce flawed or unverified patch despite reporting success - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review actual patch and tests, issue findings and approve/reject before finalization - Decision owner: Independent review-stage model agent process, distinct from execute agent; separate triage routes findings - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-reviewer separate process, role-specific prompt, immutable review files, exact-head CI gates, bounded fix-pass ledger - Closure path: Reviewer findings return to triage/fix and trigger new verification on updated exact PR head before completion - Boundary reachability: The default coding workflow includes a shipped 6-review stage with configured independent agent reviewer adapters - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed independent reviewers decide the substantive findings; Ruby rules enforce pass/merge conditions only - Claim being audited: Implementer says its branch satisfies requirements and is ready for PR finalization - Ordinary reporting path: Implementation stage marker, code commit and worktree output - Complementary access path: Independent subprocess directly inspects diff, task details, CI and project tests - Independence boundary: Fresh isolated model session and review prompt, distinct from implementing agent; models can still share provider - Who acts on findings: Triage/fix agent and final stage gate; operator decides unresolved escalations - Evidence: wiki/stages/review.md; lib/hive/reviewers.rb; docs/concepts.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Post-merge architecture observation and prospective project improvement via Patrol and Patrol Fix - Disturbance / variety regulated: Source architecture may drift, duplicate components or weaken boundaries after new features - Decisive decision or feedback right: Generate and route source-backed refactoring theses; accept or decline proposed future source changes - Decision owner: Patrol agent produces candidate options; legitimate project maintainer decides acceptance/merge in the parent-governed mode - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Merged-PR manifests, read-only architecture reviewer, evidence-checked snippets, immutable findings, separate Patrol Fix workflow - Closure path: Post-merge change drives future refactoring proposal; an accepted maintainer merge applies a new source baseline used by later project tasks - Boundary reachability: Packaged opt-in Architecture Patrol/Patrol Fix and native daemon operate on registered source repos, not test fixtures - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An agent can propose improvements but may not unilaterally claim that its PR has been adopted; parent owns final integration - External distinction: Changed default-branch source and architecture coupling revealed after completed features - Future / prospective distinction: Refactoring thesis addresses later maintainability and capability, not just active task validation - Adaptation option generated: Evidence-cited architecture thesis routed to fix/discuss/dismiss and Patrol Fix intake - Path back into current capability / S3: Maintainer acceptance and merge changes tracked default branch; subsequent task worktrees use that updated capability - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; wiki/modules/patrol.md; wiki/commands/refactor-patrol.md - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Constitute a local project&#39;s enduring workflow, role, stage approval and deployment/acceptance policy - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent autonomy and quality or publication behavior must remain within project owner&#39;s continuing purpose - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select workflow descriptor, stage permissions, gate/approval policy and persistent settings - Decision owner: Legitimate local project owner, with upstream maintainer retaining external PR acceptance authority - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: State-branch workflow YAML, validated stage permissions, protected policy snapshots, human decision markers - Closure path: Owner-authored workflow is validated/persisted; later tasks and stage agents follow that accepted policy and refuse disallowed actions - Boundary reachability: Native hive workflow commands, state branch, init and daemon load the owner-selected descriptor - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Stage agents work inside the adopted policy but cannot unilaterally replace the ultimate project purpose or publisher rights - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What product/task pipeline Hive operates, what acceptable completion means and what decisions remain with its legitimate owner - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Local project owner for workflow identity and external maintainers for upstream source acceptance - Return-to-operation path: Persisted policy and stage bindings → next task admission → runtime permission and approval enforcement - Evidence: docs/workflows.md; docs/permissions.md; docs/architecture.md - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/02393d19c9723c4d08c2fab2628035a254351efa/assessments/hi/hive-workflow-engine.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 02393d19c9723c4d08c2fab2628035a254351efa\">02393d1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T20:44:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chekusu/wanman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wanman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chekusu/wanman/tree/d3a8138de6c21981a4f1449a7b3f201e724c55c9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d3a8138de6c21981a4f1449a7b3f201e724c55c9\">d3a8138 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement delegated software/project tasks using model-selected code, CLI, research and documentation actions with tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous scoped tasks, changing repo state, test failures, tool observations, local process interruption and external CLI environment variation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a worker or specialist model chooses how to perform its assigned work and which next tool operation follows an observed result. - Decision owner: configured Claude Code/Codex model-backed dev, devops, feedback, marketing or other enabled agent process, not the JSON-RPC messaging wrapper. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentProcess run loop, runtime adapters, supervisor relay, per-agent worktrees/homes, task notifications and durable stores. - Closure path: assigned goal/task → worker model/tool invocation → file/artifact/event result → subsequent model turn or run completion → shared task/result updates. - Boundary reachability: documented wanman takeover and wanman run local entrypoints start first-party supervisor with real child CLI adapters; agent subprocesses are not merely illustrative external examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model workers choose substantive task solutions; deterministic startup, persistence and CLI routing implement rather than replace that choice. - Evidence: README local run/takeover contract; docs/architecture.md sections 2, 3, 7–8; supervisor.ts; takeover-project.ts role prompts. - Basis: structural exact-ref model/observation/action wiring; not inferred from a role label alone. - Confidence: high for the shipped execution path, not for performance in live unbounded tasks. - Caveats: external Claude/Codex credentials and executables are required; no live model execution was conducted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent worker interference on overlapping project file scopes and conflicting change-capsule paths. - Disturbance / variety regulated: separately operating dev agents attempting simultaneous commitments on the same path or high-conflict hot area, causing lost edits and merge collisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reject new overlapping active task scopes and high-conflict capsule plans before a second competing work commitment is accepted. - Decision owner: constructor-authored TaskPool/ChangeCapsulePool overlap rules, enforced by supervisor RPC admission; not a model-negotiated conflict adjudication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: path/prefix matching against nonterminal tasks, high-conflict capsule classification, task assignment notification, declared task dependencies and isolated worktrees. - Closure path: proposed task/capsule scope → query active peer scopes → overlap classification → reject with explicit conflicting IDs or accept/assign → subsequent workers receive only admitted scoped work. - Boundary reachability: overlap check occurs in shipped supervisor TASKCREATE/capsule RPC paths used by local takeover; it is not a separate uncalled helper or merely recommended etiquette. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed collision rules enforce bounded eligibility; the CEO/worker models can select scopes but cannot override an RPC conflict response through ordinary creation. - Distinct S1 units: separate agent subprocesses/roles operating in per-agent worktrees and receiving assigned project tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing proposed task/capsule edits to the same path or protected hot file can undermine each other&#39;s results. - Attenuating coordination relation: active-scope exclusion and high-conflict capsule rejection at shared project admission, with weaker conflicts surfaced as warnings. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected proposal returns a concrete conflict error to the submitting model, which must re-scope, defer or select different work; accepted assignment notifies its assignee. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it detects a specific collision between distinct worker scopes and changes whether conflicting work is authorized, beyond carrying messages between agents. - Evidence: task-pool.ts scopesOverlap/checkConflict; supervisor.ts TASKCREATE and CAPSULECREATE branches; change-capsule-pool.ts; takeover-project.ts capsule instructions. - Basis: first-party deterministic enforcement, bound to declared paths and admitted active records. - Confidence: high for the collision gate; medium for coverage of all real git conflicts. - Caveats: empty/inaccurate declared scopes, concurrent admission races or out-of-band editing can evade these checks; no global merge-safety guarantee is credited.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise current multi-agent project work, prioritize and revise the active task and initiative portfolio, assign owners and request rework. - Disturbance / variety regulated: idle workers, unresolved tasks, missed dependencies, changing present backlog, stale initiatives and active quality/PR blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a configured CEO model chooses near-term initiatives, task subdivision, priority, worker assignment and corrective follow-up from a shared current work view. - Decision owner: packaged model-backed ceo role in the documented takeover mode; supervisor stores and routes its decisions and optional CTO reviews particular PRs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: InitiativeBoard, TaskPool with statuses and dependencies, shared artifacts/context, health/agent reports, priority steer/follow-up, takeover mission seed/agent instructions. - Closure path: CEO reads project docs/tasks/worker results → creates/revises initiatives and bounded assignments → supervisor enforces and notifies S1 agents → completion and exception messages return to CEO for next-cycle decisions. - Boundary reachability: takeover-project.ts installs a continuously active CEO with explicit wanman initiative/task/capsule workflow, while takeover-coordination.ts addresses it with mission/repair messages through the actual supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the CEO&#39;s model makes discretionary current-management judgments; SQLite stores and deterministic eligibility checks only implement portions of the work plan. - Whole-system current view: current project agent list, initiative/task/capsule commitments and assigned outcomes accessible through the single matrix supervisor, not all of a customer&#39;s unrelated organizations. - Current-control decision scope: active project decomposition, worker allocation and current correction within externally configured permissions and user mission; ultimate policy authority is not inferred. - Evidence: takeover-project.ts CEO instructions and role config; takeover-coordination.ts mission dispatch; supervisor.ts task, initiative, capsule and agent RPC; docs/architecture.md shared stores. - Basis: a packaged operational configuration with concrete observation, decision and task return paths. - Confidence: medium-high for reachable first-party mode, not verified unattended performance. - Caveats: bare custom matrices without the CEO role may have no comparable S3; project/operator remains the policy source.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: independent challenge of normal management/agent claims with a complementary observation path to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: faulty self-report, concealed code regressions, inaccurate PR/test claims and inconsistent inter-agent evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate auditor tests operational facts and sends a correction demand to S3 independent of ordinary task-report owners. - Decision owner: no first-party separately independent audit owner is established; the CEO itself performs packaged post-completion consistency checks, and CTO is in the ordinary current-work quality flow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cross-validation skill, CTO PR review/coverage gate, work logs, scoped capsules and artifact metadata. - Closure path: routine CEO/CTO review may create corrective tasks but is part of the same project&#39;s current operating management, without a distinct complementary audit challenge channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model can check outputs, but the separately independent audit access/authority required for S3 is not evidenced by another prompt title or a recorded test outcome. - Evidence: packages/core/skills/cross-validation/SKILL.md; takeover-project.ts CTO/CEO instructions; docs/architecture.md logs and capsule stores. - Basis: independence/return-path comparison at the chosen project recursion. - Confidence: medium-high for reviewed standard local modes. - Caveats: a deployer could add an independent auditor, but no such custom augmentation is credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: examine prospective external ecosystem changes and feed strategic adaptation options into present project capability decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future market, user and technology shifts that would make current project priorities/architecture unviable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: commission/evaluate future-environment alternatives and propose a capability change for decision by current management. - Decision owner: no bundled first-party project-level S4 owner established; the feedback role scans present issue/docs/backlog signals and the CEO extends the current roadmap. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: feedback/marketing roles, issue and README scanning, hypothesis/initiative records and an implementation-option ExplorationEngine module. - Closure path: feedback scans existing repo signals and suggests backlog tasks; implementation-option engine addresses a supplied implementation goal, not a materially wired external-future environmental interpretation/return channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model&#39;s present backlog discovery or solution-option search does not itself entail a separate outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: takeover-project.ts feedback/marketing and CEO instructions; takeover-mission.ts seeds; exploration-engine.ts and workflow-orchestrator.ts. - Basis: inspected configured role missions and absence of a credited prospective project S3–S4 closure. - Confidence: medium; a suitable user-created agent matrix may add this, beyond the credited package configuration. - Caveats: the project may support prospective research tasks in principle; assessed ownership requires a concrete standard-mode function rather than prompt potential.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: legitimate settlement of ultimate project identity and policy disputes and authoritative return of the decision to the rest of the matrix. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting foundational goals, forbidden actions, mission legitimacy or unresolved current-versus-future organizational policy tension. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an accepted constitutional authority decides whether and how to redefine the project mandate, not merely whether one task is approved. - Decision owner: external human/project owner sets mission, runtime credentials, allowed operations and action approvals; the local named CEO is a current-work planner, not self-authorized ultimate policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supplied mission and AGENT.md, static safety-gate approvals, role permissions, external operator commands and mission-seed update. - Closure path: human can change mission/config or approve a restricted action, which influences later runs, but no first-party identity-dispute detection/adjudication/return loop is wired for the declared project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the label CEO, task priorities, action approvals and external user configuration do not establish a legitimate S5 arbitration mandate. - Evidence: takeover-project.ts mission construction/CEO scope, safety-gate.ts human approvals, README operator/runtime configuration. - Basis: mapped policy ownership and compared ordinary execution permission with identity-level closure. - Confidence: medium-high for standard local operation. - Caveats: the human operator may be part of a larger viable S5 outside wanman; this is not an evidenced integrated parent P feedback closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/81f31493390b597441cf2729e6167f6138178ff1/assessments/wa/wanman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 81f31493390b597441cf2729e6167f6138178ff1\">81f3149 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T17:51:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rizwan3d/StemCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">StemCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rizwan3d/StemCode/tree/8e52bb02310b91337bf6eb88e163fc65acb059f8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8e52bb02310b91337bf6eb88e163fc65acb059f8\">8e52bb0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing software-engineering work by interpreting the current objective, selecting permitted inspection/edit/shell/validation actions, executing them and revising subsequent actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository state, implementation alternatives, provider uncertainty, build/test/tool failures, incomplete plans, malformed/empty provider outputs and task-specific evidence discovered during execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific model/tool action and revise that choice after observing tool/provider/workspace evidence. - Decision owner: the active model-backed StemCode primary actor; delegated child actors own the same local S1 discretion for their bounded task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/execution pipeline, active profile, permission overlays, session persistence, provider adapters, plan/loop guards, workspace instructions/memory, tracked file-edit transactions and interrupt/recovery handling. - Closure path: task/current session state → provider/model decision → first-party tool execution → result persisted and returned to the conversation → same actor selects another action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the first-party AgentConversationPipeline and built-in profiles are the standard runtime used by the documented CLI/Desktop/editor/headless product surfaces; no adjacent development actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining tools, permissions, sessions and deterministic guards removes the task-specific choice of what to inspect, edit, run or validate next; those mechanisms constrain or transport rather than replace that discretion. - Evidence: AgentConversationPipeline.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: particular tools may require operator approval or be denied by profile/policy, but supported operation retains autonomous task-level decision/action loops.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among subordinate S1 units when a primary StemCode actor coordinates several bounded child tasks against the same workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous editing-capable subagents could overwrite, revert or otherwise conflict with one another in the shared repository; unconstrained delegated scopes can also create cross-task churn. - Distinct S1 units: separate child ReplSessionContext executions created by agentorchestrate, each running the first-party conversation pipeline under general or explore and returning its own tool/output/edit evidence. - Inter-S1 disturbance: editing-capable child actors share the parent workspace and can therefore contend on repository mutations; StemCode&#39;s own orchestration contract distinguishes these from read-only tasks and explicitly warns coordinated agents not to revert others&#39; changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: the primary model chooses the child task set, subagent profiles, execution strategy and optional write scopes; auto then parallelizes consecutive read-only tasks but executes editing-capable tasks in controlled sequence, while parallelreadonly rejects mutating subagents entirely. Delegated prompts transmit task/write-scope/coordination constraints to each S1. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: strategy and write-scope choices alter when child S1s run and what workspace region they are instructed to touch; each child handoff/tool/edit result returns to the parent actor, which can integrate the evidence into subsequent operation or another coordinated handoff. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation is explicitly conditioned on the concrete interference class of shared-workspace mutations. Read-only work is allowed to overlap; editing-capable work is deliberately serialized or rejected from the parallel mode, and bounded write scopes are passed to mutating children. This is targeted disturbance attenuation, not sequencing merely because tasks exist. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which bounded child activities should coexist, which agent profile should perform each, what coordination strategy to use and what write boundaries to assign before those S1s operate. - Decision owner: the primary model-backed StemCode actor owns the task-specific coordination discretion; deterministic orchestration code enforces the nonparallel mutation safety rule and concurrency limit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentOrchestrateTool; child-session construction; MaxParallelReadOnlyTasks; editing-capable classification; strategy validation; semaphore; sequential execution of mutating requests; delegated coordination/write-scope prompt; returned aggregate result. - Closure path: primary actor identifies independent/dependent side work → calls agentorchestrate with tasks/strategy/scopes → runtime schedules read-only parallelism and mutation serialization → child S1s receive coordination constraints and operate → their results/edit evidence return to the primary actor → later primary/child behaviour can be revised. - Boundary reachability: agentorchestrate is in the standard build profile and in supported inspection-oriented primary profiles where applicable; child general/explore profiles and the execution policy are shipped first-party runtime behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the primary actor is removed, deterministic code can still serialize a pre-existing request, but no task-specific actor remains to decide the coordinated task set, child profiles, strategy or write scopes. The runtime owns enforcement; the primary agent owns the bounded coordination choice. - Evidence: AgentOrchestrateTool.cs; AgentDelegationSupport.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this S2 is bounded to one orchestration handoff and the shared-workspace interference it regulates; it does not imply persistent organization-wide current control.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S3 whole-current control loop was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task decomposition, active-plan tracking, permission enforcement, orchestration batch scheduling and token/budget limits exist, but no distinct whole-system current-operation variety is shown being regulated through S3 authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live plan/update-plan machinery, bounded subagent orchestration, permission profiles, budget controls, session state, interrupts and deterministic concurrency limits. - Closure path: absent at S3 level; no first-party persistent whole-system current view → shared-resource/commitment/priority intervention → returned change to an independently evolving operational population was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the primary model does allocate bounded delegated work, but that is parent-task decomposition inside one awaited orchestration call. The reviewed path does not expose the whole-system current view and current-control authority required by the Profile. - Evidence: AgentOrchestrateTool.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; AgentConversationPipeline.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future/background-task machinery could change this conclusion if it supplied a population view plus substantive intervention rights; that path is not established in the frozen revision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide an audit-specific, read-only review path over a proposed change set that is separate from the ordinary implementation actor and can challenge its claims with findings about bugs, regressions, unsafe assumptions, edge cases and missing tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty that the normal implementation/reporting path may miss defects or weak tests in a PR/change set. - Claim being audited: that a proposed repository change is correct/safe enough with no material unreported regressions or testing gaps. - Ordinary reporting path: the ordinary coding actor edits/runs validation and reports completion through its primary session/tool loop; routine build/test results remain part of that production path. - Complementary access path: shipped GitHub review automation computes the independent base/head diff, invokes a separate read-only pr-reviewer profile against that artifact and publishes findings as a PR review/comment, with an uploaded review artifact. - Independence boundary: the reviewer is a separately invoked StemCode session/profile with read-only/safe-inspection permissions and receives the PR diff rather than inheriting the implementation actor&#39;s conversational claims. However the committed workflow&#39;s PR trigger is commented out and the path does not autonomously drive corrective execution back into the ordinary coding actor. - Who acts on findings: the hosting-platform/user/developer workflow consuming the posted PR findings; first-party StemCode does not in this frozen standard setup automatically convert those findings into a corrective implementation turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party tooling exposes the audit-specific independent judgment and outbound findings path, but the autonomous corrective authority/return closure must still be composed by the adopter. - Decision owner: constructor state. StemCode supplies the independent model-backed reviewer and findings publication primitive; deployment wiring and downstream corrective authority remain outside the closed autonomous product loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pr-reviewer read-only profile; base/head diff construction; review script; GitHub workflow permissions/artifact upload; PR comment/review publication. - Closure path: first-party path reaches PR findings publication but stops before autonomous corrective control; an adopter must compose the findings-consumer/repair return path for full closure. - Boundary reachability: the audit path is explicitly shipped and documented as StemCode CI review automation with first-party scripts/profile/workflow, so the S3-specific constructor is reachable without borrowing a third-party reviewer. The missing autonomous corrective return is why the state is C, not A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment itself is made by a separate model-backed reviewer, not by a deterministic parser. The incomplete part is organizational closure from findings back into corrective operation, so the assessment does not publish A. - Evidence: .github/stemcode-github-review.sh; .github/workflows/stemcode-review.yml; .stemcode/agents/pr-reviewer.md; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: repository co-location alone is not used as evidence. The positive state is intentionally only the shipped audit-specific constructor path; ordinary review profile use inside the same production conversation and routine tests are not credited as S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop over StemCode&#39;s own current capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web search, codebase intelligence, repository/team memory and optional lessons expose useful information for task execution and reuse, but they do not establish S4 at the assessed organizational recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: websearch, headless browser, codebase/LSP tools, repo memory, lesson memory, automatic tool-failure observation, skills and configurable profiles/providers. - Closure path: no external/future distinction → adaptation-option development → return into present organizational capability/S3 loop was established. Lesson retrieval returns prior local mistakes/fixes to future prompts but is not an external-and-prospective adaptation conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can use web/repository evidence for the current task and can save/retrieve lessons, but that is current-task sensing/learning unless tied to future-oriented organizational adaptation of capability. - Evidence: BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; StemCode/Application/Tools/LessonMemoryTool.cs; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future support for agent-owned capability/profile/tool adaptation driven by environmental/future modeling could establish S4; current reusable memory alone does not.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, profiles, workspace instructions, budgets, approval prompts and operator configuration constrain ordinary operation, but no S5-level identity/policy issue is adjudicated through a runtime ultimate-authority loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established at S5; developers/operators author configuration and ordinary approval choices outside any qualifying first-party S5 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in profile permission overlays; allow/deny/ask rules; workspace files/instructions; provider/model configuration; budget controls; operator prompts and runtime enforcement. - Closure path: absent at S5 level; no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing subsequent operation path was found in the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model is constrained by configured policy but does not own the ultimate right to redefine StemCode&#39;s identity or top-level policy; static configuration and ordinary tool approval are specifically insufficient for S5. - Evidence: BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; README.md; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository maintainer governance is adjacent to the product boundary and is not imported as runtime S5 merely because it determines future releases.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/52c1740f0432c2d27847af8501de8f2772f55a86/assessments/st/stemcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 52c1740f0432c2d27847af8501de8f2772f55a86\">52c1740 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T07:47:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nexu-io/looper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Looper</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nexu-io/looper/tree/efc199d6d584e9cffca3464e190f060cc1b71f39\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision efc199d6d584e9cffca3464e190f060cc1b71f39\">efc199d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously select model-driven repository planning, coding/repair and PR review operations within the offered first-party role workflows. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing issue requirements, source context, failures, test outcomes and PR-review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: activated role agent chooses analysis, implementation or repair actions from task/prompts and observed Git/PR state; the configured external AI CLI supplies the model/tool execution substrate. - Decision owner: first-party configured role agent when invoked through a supported installed vendor; the third-party runtime implementation is execution substrate, not an extra Looper S-system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-specific prompts, Git worktrees, local daemon queue, GitHub/Forgejo integration, lease containment and persisted loop state. - Closure path: the role reads task or review input, produces spec/PR changes or review findings, publishes them through the supported forge and receives subsequent observed state to continue, retry or finish. - Boundary reachability: daemon&#39;s Worker/Planner/Reviewer/Fixer runners use the shipped vendor executor under configured agent vendor, including manual role mode, as documented in the first-party CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven role chooses substantive content and edits, while daemon gates eligible admission and subprocess containment. A vendor CLI must be installed/configured; no separate ownership claim is made for unsupported vendor capabilities. - Evidence: loop roles and termination criteria, supported role agents, agent subprocess Start, worker runner. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: a distinct coordination decision right attenuating concrete inter-S1 oscillation/interference is not established beyond deterministic claim/lease isolation and role handoff at the reviewed root. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous loops might compete for a PR/issue/worktree, but source paths largely avoid duplicates with static eligibility and queue fencing rather than model-governed mutual adjustment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no positive S2-specific first-party owner/constructor path established. - Decision owner: daemon deterministic scheduler and configured GitHub/Forgejo claim/eligibility policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: queue claims, worktree isolation, reviewer/fixer handoff labels and network lease validation. - Closure path: queue gates can block duplicate starts and role sequencing advances; no independent S2-specific control decision and feedback among competing S1 agents is credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer findings closing into Fixer changes are assessed as independent audit, not automatically S2; sequential role workflow is insufficient to prove an autonomous inter-worker conflict regulator. - Evidence: roles handoff and worktrees, scheduler claim/lease enforcement, worker claim collision guard. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent-operator current control of the daemon&#39;s active portfolio via inspection and real-time interruption, release, retry and takeover of ongoing role loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked role loops, unresolved review/fix budget holds, stalled work, manual scope decisions, improper ongoing commitments and competing operator priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: which current loop to pause, resume, terminate, take over or restart, after inspecting the daemon&#39;s active and blocked state. - Decision owner: legitimate local operator in the supported parent-controlled mode; deterministic code executes chosen controls. No separate autonomous A or constructor C S3 mode established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI looper ps, looper loop inspect, queue diagnostics, persisted LoopRecord, service Pause/Terminate/Resume methods and scheduler requeue. - Closure path: operator reads loop/queue current state, issues looper unpause, stop or takeover action; the first-party service durably changes loop status, cancels or resumes agent execution and scheduler uses the new state on subsequent dispatch. - Boundary reachability: standard shipped local daemon/CLI supports status, ps, stop, unpause, takeover list/stop as real operator pathways; no external custom plugin is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: review/fix agents do not decide whole-fleet current priorities; the local parent operator does, with real return into active queue/role execution. - Whole-system current view: looper ps, loop inspect, queue failed and takeover list jointly expose active and blocked loops over registered projects, run/agent status, failure/hold and current assigned PR. - Current-control decision scope: current assignment/commitment intervention (pause, hold release, stop, terminate, force/manual takeover, retry) and practical resource engagement by canceling or resuming active jobs. - Evidence: operator commands, pause/resume/terminate service, diagnostic portfolio API, takeover controls. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: this is a parent-governed supported mode, not S3=A. A kill button alone would not suffice; here live portfolio/hold visibility and returned multi-loop commitment interventions are the credited combined surface.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Reviewer role independently inspects actual new PR head/diff and acceptance criteria, judges findings and returns them to the separate Fixer/Worker operational loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: claimed completed implementation may hide bugs, missed requirements or maintainability flaws not visible in Worker self-report/tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer agent independently selects substantive review findings for the current PR and whether actionable issues remain; Fixer consumes review threads and modifies code, triggering a subsequent fresh review. - Decision owner: first-party configured Reviewer role/model, separate from implementation Worker/Fixer; deterministic review marker, criteria parser and runner do not become the audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PR snapshot/head tracking, separate read of patch and criteria, issue/PR review API, thread state, review scheduler, worktree and optional isolation. - Closure path: Reviewer reads actual PR code and acceptance criteria, posts actionable review findings; Fixer changes/pushes new head in response; Reviewer re-reads changed head and continues until no actionable threads remain or a human hold is needed. - Boundary reachability: shipped Reviewer/Fixer loops and supported manual looper review ... --loop with distinct role prompts and configured model are first-party selectable code paths, not an external unselected third-party auditor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer agent judges what needs improvement; automatic event markers, static validators and human terminal merge are support/ultimate approval, not replacements for the independent audit judgment. - Claim being audited: implementation is complete, matches task/spec and contains no actionable code defects. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker/Fixer implementation PR, model-generated completion markers, test/check statuses and changed commit. - Complementary access path: Reviewer independently retrieves current PR head, diff and reviewable source/context through GitHub/Forgejo, rather than accepting Worker completion assertions. - Independence boundary: dedicated Reviewer role and its independent PR read/analysis loop are distinct from the implementation Writer/Fixer task; separate prompts/eligibility and repeat review on new commits. Same configured vendor provider may be used, so model-identity independence is not claimed. - Who acts on findings: Fixer loop addresses actionable review threads; Reviewer rechecks after changes; unresolved issues may be held for human/operator. - Evidence: reviewer/fixer loop contract, reviewer evidence/criteria processing, reviewer stage/re-evaluation, fixer review-thread feedback, reviewer head-change detection. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped loop builds a prospective external/environment view, selects future adaptation options for Looper&#39;s own operating capability and returns decisions to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new issue assignments, commit notifications and provider errors drive present jobs; they are not an explicitly prospective external-change judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S4. - Decision owner: configured role/runtime and repository developer/operator update Looper&#39;s code, vendors and policy outside the evaluated runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: roadmap/ADR documents, scheduled issue polling, provider capability inspection, upgrade commands and retry policies. - Closure path: external issue events can trigger present coding jobs and provider settings can be manually updated; no future-adaptation judgment is brought back into current Looper capability by a first-party S4 actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Planner plans a future task implementation, but not how the harness itself adapts to an external prospective environment. - Evidence: role purpose, configurable agents, scheduler. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: live ultimate-policy or organizational identity closure is not established within the installed Looper daemon&#39;s standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: PR merge permissions, human intervention, vendor access, privacy and tenancy are handled through static configuration and forge/host authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped identity or ultimate-policy decision with authoritative feedback into subsequent operations. - Decision owner: local operator and source repository/forge administration define policies; agents operate within them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GitHub/Forgejo merge requirements, per-role agent selection, static config, label workflows, leases and escalation prompts. - Closure path: a user may approve merge or take over a PR, which closes a particular current task, not a S5 system identity issue returned as an operative ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: review decisions concern code quality and present execution; they neither grant nor choose top-level purpose/identity, and ordinary human merge permission is not S5=P. - Evidence: human merge boundary, configured operator/workflow gates, role/daemon configuration. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7b23079366ab806ab6a1f0d7d753c79c1588dff0/assessments/lo/looper-nexu.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7b23079366ab806ab6a1f0d7d753c79c1588dff0\">7b23079 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T19:15:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/witt3rd/oh-my-hermes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Oh My Hermes</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/witt3rd/oh-my-hermes/tree/3b06e33ae67b202b342989b484f08143e65e250d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3b06e33ae67b202b342989b484f08143e65e250d\">3b06e33 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement and verify individual software-plan tasks using model-guided agents and actual project tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain implementation paths, changing repository files, build/test failures, unexpected tool results and acceptance feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what code/commands to use, revise an implementation after verifier feedback and produce task artifacts. - Decision owner: Hermes parent/executor model agents invoked by first-party OMH ralph/autopilot skill modes; external Hermes supplies the model runtime, not the organizational task choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegatetask, OMH role hooks, work-state persistence, planned acceptance criteria, evidence command execution and progress logs. - Closure path: a plan task is delegated to an executor; source changes and tool feedback are returned; the separate verdict can cause targeted rework, task completion or a blocked state, changing subsequent operating action. - Boundary reachability: README documents skill and optional plugin installation; omh-ralph/SKILL.md explicitly invokes a provider-backed delegated executor within that supported installed mode, while plugin prellmcall injects packaged executor role context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects implementation actions under task/role constraints; plugin code only stores and enforces the workflow. - Evidence: Ralph Steps 3–6, role injection hook, delegation boundary. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for supported skill mode, not actual task quality. - Caveats: requires a separately installed Hermes runtime/model and user/caller re-invocation; no live provider-backed workflow was executed for this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate collisions among parallel task workers and between two live OMH sessions targeting the same plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent task agents writing overlapping files and separate workflow sessions racing on the same plan/state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which tasks can safely run in parallel and whether an invocation may proceed with a particular workflow instance. - Decision owner: skill-following Hermes coordinator chooses independent task groups and responds to lock-denied feedback; deterministic OMH state tool implements the keyed lock and refuses conflicting holders. The full function requires explicit installed skill/plugin composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-instance advisory locks in omhstate, key/holder records, Ralph and Autopilot acquisition/release discipline, dependency/file-footprint checks and cancellation. - Closure path: before work, the skill asks the state tool to lock a project plan; a conflicting holder causes wait/cancel/other-plan feedback rather than two writers proceeding on the same plan. Within a run, the coordinator selects only independent tasks for parallel delegation and feeds results into later task choice. - Boundary reachability: both installed omh-ralph and omh-autopilot explicitly call shipped omhstate(action=&quot;lock&quot;), and the registered statetool.py dispatches to lock acquire/release/check; no hypothetical third-party coordination plugin is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C records the explicitly composed agent decision plus state-based contention mechanism; fixed mutex implementation alone is not promoted to an autonomous S2 decision owner. - Distinct S1 units: parallel executor child agents within Ralph; competing skill-driven workflow sessions for one plan form the second, narrower interference case. - Inter-S1 disturbance: same-file overlap or simultaneous updates to the same .omh task/plan state. - Attenuating coordination relation: dependence and file-footprint screening before parallel delegates, plus keyed plan/session lock and conflict response. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a denied lock pauses/reroutes a would-be session; only noninterfering workers receive parallel tasks, and their reports affect the coordinator&#39;s next eligible-task selection. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path addresses explicit competing writer/session interference, not mere message delivery or parent-to-child sequencing; the state lock is connected to the skill&#39;s alternate action on contention. - Evidence: Ralph lock and parallel selection, Autopilot contention response, state tool, state engine. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: advisory rather than universally enforced across independent Hermes instances; plugin-free skills and arbitrary independent writers do not inherit this guarantee. Coordination within unrelated projects is not assessed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current whole-workflow task commitments, retries, completion and failure handling across delegated workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck plan dependencies, incomplete acceptance criteria, repeated identical errors, unfinished tasks, conflicting runtime-phase progress and tool/test failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose eligible tasks, batch or defer work, initiate corrective retries, mark tasks passed or blocked, and determine current phase progression under shared constraints. - Decision owner: skill-following Hermes coordinating agent inside the supported Ralph/Autopilot mode; first-party state machinery tracks and enforces its choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: omhstate plan/task records, prerequisite gate, dependency graph, max-iterations and three-strike limits, omhgatherevidence, verification verdicts and cancellation. - Closure path: coordinator reads whole-plan/task status, assigns eligible work, receives executor and evidence-based verifier outcomes, writes changed pass/block/retry status, and the next invocation uses this state to alter commitments and later dispatches. - Boundary reachability: omh-ralph is a shipped installable skill with explicit Steps 1–8 and whole-plan state transitions; omh-autopilot repeatedly checks Ralph state and advances/presents blocked current work. The actor is reachable via the installed Hermes tool boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing what whole-plan work is committed, retried or stopped depends on the coordinator&#39;s context-sensitive judgment, not solely on deterministic state-file writes or raw task delegation. - Whole-system current view: active phase, plan/task queue, dependency status, retries, evidence verdicts, error fingerprints, cancellation/lock state and current invocation. - Current-control decision scope: work allocation and acceptance across all current tasks in a project workflow, not a child agent&#39;s local tool choice or future identity decision. - Evidence: Ralph orchestration and recovery, Autopilot phase controller, state engine. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user/caller still launches each separate invocation. The system does not autonomously continue if nobody re-invokes it; current decisions within a reached invocation are the claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently distinct nonroutine complementary audit of ordinary execution claims established in the supported OMH work boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an executor or coordinator can misreport success, and normal tests may miss defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifier and reviewer roles issue PASS/FAIL or REQUESTCHANGES in mandatory delivery/QA phases, but no separately controlled sporadic audit decision is established beyond that ordinary path. - Decision owner: routine Hermes verifier/reviewer and same-workflow coordinator own acceptance, not a separately evidenced S3 audit function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only verifier prompt, fresh command output, QA and parallel validation reviewers, research citation checks and plan criticism. - Closure path: failed verifier/reviewer judgments lead to executor retry or a blocked state within the normal production pipeline; a distinct complementary audit escalation/alternative observation path is not shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different model role and real fresh test output strengthen routine quality control but do not by themselves provide independent sporadic access outside the standard success-report path. - Evidence: Ralph Step 5 verification, Autopilot QA/validation, verifier role, evidence tool. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: an external independent auditor could inspect project artifacts, but that would be adjacent to rather than demonstrated inside this product mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environmental scanning and strategic capability adaptation of OMH itself established in the assessed project workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external tool/model capabilities, project environment and future requirements might require adaptation beyond the currently requested work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the shipped research skill can synthesize outside facts for a user&#39;s research or planning task, but no agent-owned mechanism determines OMH&#39;s future capability policy and returns such options into its own present operational configuration. - Decision owner: project users define research/feature goals; current-task planners execute them. OMH maintainers&#39; future roadmap is adjacent development, not in-boundary S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: omh-deep-research, omh-ralplan, persistent plans/specs, task learnings forwarded to later executors and roadmap documents. - Closure path: a topic-specific report or implementation plan can inform the next user task, but there is no established outside-and-then system self-adaptation judgment with a durable capability change owned by the runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external research as an S1 deliverable, feedback on the present coding task and stored learnings are not automatically a future-oriented S4 conversation with present capability. - Evidence: deep research scope, Ralplan purpose, Autopilot phases. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: specialized user-authored strategic workflows could be composed on top of OMH; generic possibility is not a shipped S4 ownership path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported identity- or ultimate-policy dispute/decision path established for a deployed OMH workflow as distinct from normal task authorization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential conflicts about what the multi-agent workflow ultimately exists to do and who may redefine its identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users confirm requirements or approve risky plans, but no shipped ultimate identity-policy judgment is routed to a legitimate S5 owner and returned as governing operating policy. - Decision owner: normal local user for specifications/cancellation and upstream project maintainers for product evolution; neither has a documented in-boundary S5 loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: interview confirmation, Ralplan consensus, triage sign-off, command allowlists, plugin configuration and cancellation. - Closure path: these mechanisms can govern an ordinary task transition; no distinct charter/purpose dispute → legitimate policy authority → binding decision → subsequent identity-governed operation path was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static skill purpose, safety allowlist and user sign-off cannot by themselves be equated with ultimate-policy ownership. - Evidence: deep interview user-confirmed scope, triage sign-off, config. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: higher-recursion human governance may exist outside this one local workflow; it is not borrowed into this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96d75706f77a718a6173501a19ce98b9f76bceb8/assessments/oh/oh-my-hermes.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96d75706f77a718a6173501a19ce98b9f76bceb8\">96d7570 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T04:23:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/get-vix/vix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Vix</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/get-vix/vix/tree/e68a25c7df685335f5a202844988d492fe3c1b16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e68a25c7df685335f5a202844988d492fe3c1b16\">e68a25c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify software to satisfy a coding objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository state, implementation choices, tool/test failures, context variation, provider responses and bounded delegated investigations/implementation work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive code/tool actions, interpret evidence and decide how to satisfy the engineering objective. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Vix agent or a model-backed workflow/subagent step owning its bounded operational outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon tool dispatch, sandbox/permission checks, workflow engine, session state, provider adapters, timeouts and budgets. - Closure path: objective → agent selects coding/tool action → runtime executes and returns evidence → agent revises work until completion/blocking. - Boundary reachability: the standard interactive agent, workflow agent steps and scheduled headless runs all invoke the shipped first-party model/tool runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic machinery carries/enforces actions; the model chooses the substantive engineering response. - Evidence: README.md; internal/config/defaults/agents/general.md; internal/daemon/subagent.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: tools and workflows constrain the action surface without owning the coding judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material standard-distribution path is established that specifically attenuates an evidenced interference/oscillation between distinct operational S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying inter-S1 disturbance is placed under a first-party coordination loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent, background task IDs, taskoutput, workflow edges/forks, per-step budgets and separate threads transport/decompose work but do not themselves regulate a concrete peer-operational collision. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models can delegate or run parallel children, but delegation/parallelism alone does not establish coordination; no standard collision/oscillation response with feedback into both/affected S1 units was found. - Distinct S1 units: independent spawned agents or workflow/job agent steps can own bounded outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous write-capable units could conflict in shared project state, but the reviewed standard path does not document a corresponding S2-specific attenuation relation. - Attenuating coordination relation: none established beyond generic sequencing/routing/thread isolation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: no S2-specific collision result/negotiation path established. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is not; the inspected primitives remain generic delegation/workflow transport for this purpose. - Evidence: internal/daemon/subagent.go; internal/daemon/toolschemas.go; internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a custom workflow can serialize work, but static workflow ordering is not sufficient S2 evidence under the active Profile.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material autonomous or constructor S3 current-control loop is established at the declared standard boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no whole-system current operational disturbance is placed under a first-party supervisory decision right. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow budgets/conditions, job scheduler state, timeout/auto-disable, task lists and user plan approval enforce or expose selected constraints but do not supply the required whole-system current-control discretion. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary agents own local task execution; deterministic workflow/scheduler machinery owns no discretionary whole-system S3 judgment, and the Plan approval gate occurs before execution rather than regulating a live operational whole. - Whole-system current view: job/thread/workflow status surfaces exist, but no standard autonomous owner is wired to use them as a whole-system current-control view. - Current-control decision scope: no qualifying standard owner/path established over current resource/commitment/priorities on behalf of the whole. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json; internal/daemon/jobs/scheduler.go; internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users can approve plans and manage jobs; those local/configuration interventions are not promoted to S3 without the Profile&#39;s whole-system current-control closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently audit an operational agent&#39;s completion claim against current project state and return gaps into corrective execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, missing requirements, unverifiable implementation or defects that ordinary worker self-report can miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make an independent pass/fail audit judgment from direct project evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed verify/reviewer agent running in a distinct workflow step/context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal workflow routing, structured JSON verdict, read/bash/code-intelligence tool access and deterministic pass/fail branch. - Closure path: pursue agent signals complete → separate verify agent independently inspects current state → verifier returns pass/fail plus feedback → fail routes feedback into a new pursue iteration; pass allows wrap-up. - Boundary reachability: Goal is a bundled standard workflow and its verifier/pursue routing is shipped in the default workflow configuration; reviewer agent/prompt is bundled first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: workflow code routes the result, but the separate model-backed auditor makes the evidence-based judgment. - Claim being audited: the operational worker&#39;s claim that the requested goal is complete. - Ordinary reporting path: pursue&#39;s completion signal and ordinary implementation/tool results. - Complementary access path: a separate verifier/reviewer can read current repository state, run checks and form its own evidence-based verdict rather than trusting the worker transcript. - Independence boundary: distinct workflow agent step/reviewer role with its own run context and evidence-gathering tools; the Goal verify prompt is explicitly an independent audit. - Who acts on findings: deterministic workflow routing returns rejected-audit feedback to the operational pursue agent, which must address the gaps before signalling completion again. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json; internal/config/defaults/prompts/goal/verify.md; internal/config/defaults/agents/reviewer.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S3 is credited to the independent evidence path and corrective return, not merely to the name &quot;reviewer&quot; or to ordinary self-tests.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: construct recurring external/future sensing workflows whose model-backed reaction can develop and apply adaptation to the current project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future time-based needs and changing external conditions such as new pull requests, dependency/security changes, remote state or other polled events. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the composed scheduled agent step can interpret newly sensed external evidence and choose an adaptive project response; the standard distribution exposes the sensing, gating, model-run and unattended-write path, while the concrete adaptation objective/authority is supplied by the composed job/workflow. - Decision owner: constructor state — Vix supplies the function-specific first-party primitives and an autonomous general-agent runtime, but the developer/operator/creating agent must compose the concrete external distinction, prospective objective and return policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon cron/at scheduler, prompt resolution, bash polling, executeif gating, workflow agent steps, auto-write permissions, timeout/budget controls and persisted job state. - Closure path: composed external/time trigger → first-party polling/sensing step → conditional model-backed agent interprets the changed environment and develops a response → unattended workflow may write/act in project cwd → subsequent project capability/operation changes. - Boundary reachability: the jobs skill and scheduler are shipped first-party; jobs run through the daemon as isolated headless Vix threads and can invoke named/inline workflows using the standard general agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the eventual reacting agent can be autonomous, but the standard distribution does not pre-close one concrete external-and-prospective adaptation conversation; composition supplies the environmental model/objective/authority, so the published base state is C. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md; internal/daemon/jobs/scheduler.go; internal/daemon/jobrunner.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a reminder or event reaction alone is not S4; C rests on the first-party watcher/workflow construction path being specifically capable of sensing external/future distinctions, invoking an autonomous option-developing agent and returning action into project capability. - External distinction: the polling recipe explicitly senses changes outside the current coding run, such as newly opened pull requests or analogous remote/project-environment events. - Future / prospective distinction: cron/at triggers and persisted jobs defer sensing and model judgment to later environmental states rather than only reacting inside the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: the conditionally invoked general agent can interpret the new external evidence and develop a project response, such as a risk treatment or code/configuration change, under the composed workflow objective. - Path back into current capability / S3: the scheduled agent runs in the target project cwd with workflow/tool authority (including unattended writes when enabled), so a composed adaptation decision can change current project capability/operation; Vix supplies this return path while the concrete S4 loop remains constructor-composed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level conflict/proposal is routed through authoritative S5 closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sandbox, deny lists, workflow denytools, permission/confirmation surfaces, prompts and configuration constrain local operation without becoming identity authority. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents operate within developer/operator-authored policy and cannot authoritatively redefine Vix&#39;s identity or ultimate governing principles. - Evidence: README.md; internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md; internal/daemon/sandbox.go. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human confirmation and permissions are operational safety decisions, not S5 by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ee4c77759afcb8c999e46092bd300973e782fced/assessments/vix.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ee4c77759afcb8c999e46092bd300973e782fced\">ee4c777 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/ClawGUI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ClawGUI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/ClawGUI/tree/d990d3e3f2390ced708771d9681d3c4f9a6cbef5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d990d3e3f2390ced708771d9681d3c4f9a6cbef5\">d990d3e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: execute user-requested GUI/device outcomes through autonomous perception, planning/tool orchestration and screen actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing UI state, device/application differences, task instructions, failed actions and environmental interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven agent actors choose operational plans/actions and adapt to observed device state. - Decision owner: autonomous brain/GUI-agent actor in the first-party deployment path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: device adapters, sessions, memory, chat surfaces and skill retrieval/injection. - Closure path: actions change device state, subsequent screen observations return to the agent and drive later actions until completion/failure. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - The two-agent brain/phone architecture separates responsibilities and tool execution, but the pinned evidence does not establish a concrete inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation plus a mutual-adjustment loop that attenuates it. Orchestration/delegation alone is not S2. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The brain orchestrates a task and the runtime manages sessions/device execution, but no distinct first-party actor is evidenced with current whole-organization visibility plus discretionary authority over shared resources, priorities or commitments across multiple operational S1s. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A. - Claim being audited: whether the operating PhoneAgent actually achieved the user&#39;s GUI task and, when not, where observable execution first departed from success. - Ordinary reporting path: executor rollout/actions and its normal task outcome. - Complementary access path: an explicitly independent verifier receives only sealed external evidence — screenshots, action log, accessibility/DOM data, final state and optional prior assertions — and never sees the executor&#39;s reasoning, plans or skill package. - Independence boundary: the verifier is instructed to judge only observable evidence and cannot modify the executor&#39;s playbook itself. - Decisive judgment / owner: the verifier model independently determines task success, failure type/step, root cause and behavioral corrections. - Supporting mechanisms: strict JSON schema, evidence packaging and restricted skill-file tools. - Closure path: failed verification feeds a separate skillrevise step that is required to change the skill package; when a revision is produced, PhoneAgent immediately retries using the changed capability. - Why this is S3: the path provides materially different access to operational reality, an independent judgment boundary and explicit corrective return rather than routine executor self-checking. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - The evolve loop changes future procedural capability, but its trigger and evidence are failures from the system&#39;s own current GUI-task execution. It does not establish an external-and-prospective model of changing users, threats, tools or future conditions that develops adaptation options in a two-way S3/S4 conversation. - Self-improvement alone is therefore not promoted to S4. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - User requests, model/configuration choices, skill modes and restricted edit surfaces constrain operation, but no runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is evidenced at this recursion. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/clawgui.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T19:41:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/senweaver/SenWeaverCoding\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SenWeaverCoding</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/senweaver/SenWeaverCoding/tree/8ba81d595ba154f300428dd554d2065b9a268432\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8ba81d595ba154f300428dd554d2065b9a268432\">8ba81d5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-mediated software development from user request through file/tool operations, testing/observation and subsequent repair or response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous coding tasks, unexpected tool outputs, compiler/test failures, file contents and changed instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tools or code actions based on previous responses and produce/revise a user-facing task result. - Decision owner: the active configured model in the first-party agent/unified loop and, when selected, agentic delegated role workers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: IDE/headless CLI, provider adapters, tool registry, unified loop policy, file/shell/git tools, conversation messages and subagent runtime. - Closure path: human goal → model chooses actions → first-party tool effects → observed results in model context → next model action, repair, response or bounded stop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses tool sequence and changes its actions on feedback; the Rust executor supplies capabilities and limits rather than deterministically prescribing all task decisions. - Evidence: agent entry, unified loop and delegated agentic worker in pinned source links above. - Basis: reachable model-run source path with real tool effects and returned observations. - Confidence: medium-high for supported configuration. - Caveats: outcome quality, model credentials and live execution are not validated by this static source review. - Boundary reachability: src/agent/agent.rs invokes UnifiedLoop::run, and delegateparallel is a first-party registered tool callable from the model/tool loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate cross-worker file/edit contention and task-order conflicts during concurrent delegated coding. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agentic subagents modifying shared files, simultaneous Git updates, dirty parent workspace, dependency inversions and excessive parallelism. - Decisive decision or feedback right: isolate workers in Git worktrees, serialize base-workspace merges, preserve branches when parent is dirty, and limit/sequence DAG execution. - Decision owner: constructor-specified scheduler, file/region lock manager, worktree and merge guards, not an independently policy-setting coordination agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task dependency graph, subagent limiter, basemergelock, named worktree creation, commit/merge and salvage checks, region and buffer locks. - Closure path: concurrent worker requests → graph/limit checks and separate workspaces → bounded parallel execution → serialized guarded merge or salvaged output → merged status/failure metadata available to parent and subsequent workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S2 handles a concrete interference relation between distinct active S1 workers; guards and conditional concurrency are source-defined rules rather than model-owned autonomous negotiation. - Distinct S1 units: separately instantiated agentic model workers with their own task prompt, provider invocation, allowed tools and working directory. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits or merge attempts contaminate the same codebase and can invalidate siblings&#39; results. - Attenuating coordination relation: each worker gets an isolated named worktree where available; base-workspace merge is lock-serialized, dirty parents defer merging, and scheduler capacity/dependencies govern dispatch. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: merged output exposes per-task successes/failures, degraded fallback and merge notes; failed/dirty merges preserve branches and report conditions to the parent, altering next task execution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: worktree isolation and the per-base exclusive merge lock directly suppress simultaneous file-state interference between multiple independently coding S1s. - Evidence: src/tools/delegate/parallel.rs worktree/merge/return path, src/agent/coordination.rs, multi-agent runtime and merge module (links above). - Basis: runtime code with concrete conflict attenuation, not just agent messaging or a parallelism claim. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: when worktree creation fails workers can use a shared workspace under file locks; implementation does not prove freedom from every possible merge conflict. - Boundary reachability: model-accessible delegateparallel executes the scheduler/worktree code and returns merge/failure metadata to the calling model.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model parent makes present whole-task control decisions using tool results, delegated execution outcomes and failures to choose next steps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task feasibility, partial subagent failures, unavailable capabilities/providers, ambiguous merged results, tool timeouts and integration failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decompose or pursue a task directly, select/delegate work and merge style, inspect aggregate success/degraded status, retry or change approach before final user response. - Decision owner: top-level model agent operating the unified loop and issuing delegateparallel tool calls; deterministic scheduling/merging supports its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: conversation and tool state, task graph, registry/blackboard, MergedOutput failures, optional LLM merge, bounded tool limits and cancellation. - Closure path: parent user goal → top-level model selects task execution/delegation → scheduler runs subordinate S1 agents → merged outputs/failed-task metadata return as a tool result → parent model re-evaluates current work, acts or ends. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the top-level model&#39;s future tool selections depend on feedback about the task as a whole, whereas graph dispatch/health supervision alone are deterministic support. - Whole-system current view: aggregate subtask success/degradation, merge results/failures, tool observations and current conversation/workspace state visible to the parent for the assigned coding task. - Current-control decision scope: whether to invoke subagents, which tasks/dependencies/merge mode to request, and whether the present task needs more actions or is ready for a response. - Evidence: src/agent/agent.rs, src/agent/loop/unified.rs, src/tools/delegate/parallel.rs lines covering task submission/result serialization, src/coordinator/delegation.rs. - Basis: model ownership of current task orchestration with closed worker-result return route. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is task-scoped S3, not ownership of external product release, organization portfolio, user permissions or a guaranteed multi-agent deployment. - Boundary reachability: the registered delegation tool returns serialized merged/failure metadata to the same model-aware unified loop that determines subsequent tool calls.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: internal judges and verification provide task quality signals, but no independently bounded challenger of the parent S3 decision is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect answers, weak tool outcomes, next-turn user corrections and possible integration faults. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately challenge an S3 completion claim using independently held complementary evidence and route a finding into its corrective authority. - Decision owner: optional provider-backed next-state reward judge, LLM candidate merger and verification pipeline operate under the same runtime/configuration; independent S3 decision owner is not demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolution reward queue, previous/next conversation state, self-reflection lessons, verification pipeline and optional LLM result synthesis. - Closure path: local task result → internal judge/reflection/reward or optional merge → assistant learning/report; no demonstrated blind independent S3-audit scope and binding corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: using a second model call as a score/judge is not sufficient evidence of complementary governance independence; this is internal evaluation of the same task or output. - Evidence: src/evolution/judge.rs, reflection worker, src/coordinator/delegation.rs and src/agent/verification/pipeline.rs. - Basis: independence-and-return-path test; ordinary self-check and reward are not S3. - Confidence: medium-high for scoped absence. - Caveats: an external reviewer could be installed by a user, but that independent institution is not part of the reviewed first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: optional self-reflection/evolution learns from historical turns, but does not demonstrate ongoing prospective external-environment sensing with future capability choices for the system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated assistant mistakes, weak tool outcomes, user corrections and deteriorating local work patterns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: scan the outside future environment, select strategic adaptations beyond the present task, and return actionable options into current control. - Decision owner: internal evolution/reflection agents and constructor writeback controls for local lessons; no separately evidenced prospective S4 decision-maker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: next-state reward judge, reflection worker, lesson writeback, history/distiller and optional web-search tool for ordinary user tasks. - Closure path: past answer and next-turn outcome → reward/lesson extraction → locally stored guidance; no traced external future signals → strategic option → S3 adaptation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven past-performance reflection is real but does not on its own satisfy outward-looking future intelligence; ad hoc browser/search tools are not proof of a continuing S4 mandate. - Evidence: src/evolution/judge.rs, src/evolution/reflection/reflector.rs, src/evolution/distiller.rs, web-search registration in src/agent/agent.rs. - Basis: external-future versus retrospective internal-learning distinction. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a specific user could direct a research task, but that is S1 work within the supplied goal and not systematic own-system S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: configuration and agent operating modes specify limits but do not grant authority to redefine legitimate overall system purpose or ultimate policy conflicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider permissions, credentials, tool restrictions, risk/approval constraints, model choices and user intent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the system&#39;s binding identity and top-level normative priorities when objectives conflict, with legitimate authority over the other functions. - Decision owner: external user, repository maintainers and configured permissions/providers set the top-level purpose and rules; the runtime supplies enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user settings, tool allowlists, configured model/provider, safety boundaries, agent mode and runtime guardrails. - Closure path: external user policy/request → configured restrictions → model executes bounded task or stops; no first-party final constitutional policy adjudication. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model discretion over coding steps and optional lesson generation is not independent legitimate authority to revise the harness&#39;s identity or user mandate. - Evidence: README modes, src/agent/agent.rs configuration/tool routing, delegate tool allowed-tool/max-iteration controls. - Basis: ultimate policy-right ownership boundary. - Confidence: high for scoped absence. - Caveats: S5 may exist in a larger human-led enterprise, but is not inherited into this runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5c66295f4ba0236a889ad9dee9426cf9efdfef31/assessments/se/senweavercoding.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5c66295f4ba0236a889ad9dee9426cf9efdfef31\">5c66295 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T23:02:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/canvas-org/meta-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Meta-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/canvas-org/meta-agent/tree/28e18519eba79e0e97217989390704167c43a5c8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 28e18519eba79e0e97217989390704167c43a5c8\">28e1851 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform model-led task-solving harness execution and generate actionable revised harness programs under the optimizer&#39;s run objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous benchmark tasks, failures, noisy reward/trace outcomes and implementation defects in candidate programs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: task-facing program harness calls the configured model and chooses task response/actions; model-backed proposer diagnoses examples and writes new program/prompt/tool changes. - Decision owner: model-backed target harness in task cells, and model-backed proposer for operative candidate generation, with the external model provider furnishing computation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: benchmark adapters, task runner, proposer invocation, staging directories, validation/smoke checks and run budget. - Closure path: benchmark task → candidate model/harness actions → result/trace/reward → proposed code changes → next candidate evaluation. - Boundary reachability: CLI loop invokes task-runner and invokeproposer in its shipped epoch.py code path, with explicit backend/benchmark choices. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents choose meaningful answers and candidate edits; Python scripts validate and schedule their choices. - Evidence: README.md (harness API/CLI), loop/epoch.py, loop/proposer.py, taskrunner/program.py. - Basis: explicit/structural. - Confidence: high for wired source path, no success benchmark independently rerun. - Caveats: user supplies benchmark/model credentials; local task harnesses may operate under different target adapters.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated function-specific attenuation of disturbance among distinct simultaneously acting S1 operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple candidate harnesses can be evaluated and selected, but their score comparison is an adaptation/selection process, not inter-S1 conflict/oscillation regulation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: multi-candidate workflow persists separate results and picks the higher reward; no identified live interference/negotiation right between independent operating cells. - Decision owner: deterministic selection policy and configured proposer for candidate exploration, not a team coordination authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated candidate directories, fan-out evaluation, structured results and reward sorting. - Closure path: candidate scores feed selection of future harness, not an S2-specific interference signal returned to current colliding S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel experiments and comparison do not by themselves supply VSM coordination at the declared recursion. - Evidence: loop/epoch.py (runoneepochmulti, holdout fanout, winner selection), core/experience.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded experiment pipeline. - Caveats: downstream target harnesses could have their own multi-agent coordination, not inherited by the meta-optimizer.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate present performance across task-sample cells and control which validated harness configuration becomes the accepted whole-run champion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local candidate changes may overfit seen failures, fail smoke tests or regress aggregate task accuracy/cost. - Whole-system current view: LoopState.history, baseline/epoch score summaries and optional held-out aggregate metrics summarize current results across candidate/task evaluations. - Current-control decision scope: reject structurally invalid candidates, compare aggregate search/holdout performance and choose the next working parent/best-so-far harness under the run&#39;s configured acceptance criterion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: code computes reward, evaluates all eligible siblings and updates bestrate/parent selection; with --accept-on-holdout, holdout score can gate promotion. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party loop carrying a developer/operator-defined scoring and acceptance policy; no autonomous agent owns the decisive global acceptance criterion in the standard run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: validateconfig, smokecandidate, score aggregation, archived experience and best-rate history. - Closure path: task-cell outcomes → aggregate score/current candidate comparison → selected best parent → next proposer context and subsequent candidate run. - Boundary reachability: epoch.py calls selectparentcandidatename and updates the champion in the shipped CLI, including optional but first-party holdout acceptance mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model proposer writes possible adaptations; deterministic acceptance and experiment configuration choose the installed champion based on numeric criterion, so ownership remains constructor/control machinery rather than agent discretionary S3. - Evidence: loop/epoch.py (finalizeepoch, runoneepochmulti, winneridx), loop/state.py, loop/validate.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; selection is run-level rather than continuous production governance. - Caveats: holdout may fail and source intentionally falls back to search reward; this weakens external-validity protection, not the existence of the selection path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate independent spot inspection of actual operations with corrective feedback into S3 beyond the ordinary score/validation path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: flawed or misleading candidate results are caught by validation, smoke tests and scoring, which form normal run acceptance rather than a separate audit function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: held-out evaluation may use unseen tasks and a judge/label distinct from the proposer, but it is the configured ordinary acceptance metric rather than an independent complementary inspection channel. - Decision owner: benchmark/scoring adapters and fixed run policy; no separately independent audit judgment owner with authority to challenge S3 reports. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proposer-readable trace restrictions, holdout aggregate isolation, config validation, smoke runs and score records. - Closure path: held-out score enters the ordinary best-candidate selector; no audit finding reaches a distinct supervisory corrective path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independence of a validation split from proposer prompts does not by itself establish organizational complementary audit of current operations. - Evidence: loop/epoch.py (stripholdoutfrommeta, runevaluation, finalizeepoch), taskrunner/judgerunner.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: benchmark quality, blind independence and label reliability are external and not independently verified here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense failures in the external task/benchmark environment, formulate prospective changes to the harness decision procedure and feed viable options back to the active capability configuration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes and differences in task demand, failure patterns, judge feedback, tool/reasoning deficits and overfitting risk across unseen tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed proposer studies previous trajectories and hypotheses, writes candidate future harness programs; optional model-backed skill evolver derives generalizable process updates from cross-epoch failure/success patterns. - Decision owner: first-party configured proposer model (and optional second evolution model) for semantic adaptation options; deterministic validation/selection enforces the configured promotion criterion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: experience store, search/holdout split, validator/smoke/score ranking, staging/promoted code and versioned proposer instructions. - Closure path: sampled external task failures/traces → proposer diagnoses and writes candidate behavior changes → prospective evaluation on fresh/held-out tasks → S3 selection chooses a better parent → modified program becomes current harness for later tasks/epochs. - Boundary reachability: installed meta-agent loop directly calls invokeproposer, evaluates proposed files, and makes best candidate the parent for the next iteration. --evolve-skill optionally adds versioned proposer instruction evolution but is not needed for base S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model decides which novel changes in prompts, tools, stops, routing or code to propose from environmental evidence; Python scoring constrains acceptance but does not generate the future adaptation options. - External distinction: actual heterogeneous task outcomes, error traces and judge/label feedback reveal operating-environment demand beyond the proposer internal plan. - Future / prospective distinction: proposals modify the next harness capability and are tested on task cohorts/holdout before becoming the parent&#39;s future working configuration. - Adaptation option generated: alternative executable harness source, stopping criteria, tools/agent prompts and optional process-skill guidance. - Path back into current capability / S3: candidate validation/evaluation and best-parent selection feed the changed harness into the next epoch&#39;s installed operational program. - Evidence: README.md, loop/proposer.py (invokeproposer), loop/epoch.py (runoneepoch, runoneepochmulti), loop/skillevolver.py. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for prospective closed code path, not for measured improvement. - Caveats: adaptation is task-distribution/benchmark-bound and requires model/judge credentials; it does not imply awareness of arbitrary open-world future trends or proven transfer outside the selected benchmark.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate-policy/identity deliberation/ratification cycle for the optimizer&#39;s mission or values. - Disturbance / variety regulated: acceptance thresholds, cost budgets, allowed edit surfaces and benchmark contracts constrain evolution but are preselected by the experiment operator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CLI and benchmark configs fix objective, proposer privileges, allowed edit surfaces and selection criterion; candidate evolution is explicitly prohibited from modifying target interfaces and benchmark truth. - Decision owner: external experimenter/maintainer through supplied configuration, not agentic ultimate authority or a parent-policy issue return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: surfacelock, staging validation, benchmark adapters, proposer instructions and run args. - Closure path: option checks enforce a fixed experimental mission; no system identity issue is escalated to legitimate policy authority and returned as a governing decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent editing prompts/harness code is adaptation/operation, not authority to define its organizational purpose/ultimate policy. - Evidence: loop/proposer.py, loop/validate.py, core/benchmark.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external organizational governance does not count without a first-party identity-level return-to-operation path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1d3ec2a5c696000d9ae3edc011f44bc4e1fae799/assessments/me/meta-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1d3ec2a5c696000d9ae3edc011f44bc4e1fae799\">1d3ec2a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:15:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/darshjme/djcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DJcode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-03\">2026-10-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/darshjme/djcode/tree/d915b89db1b04cca88c2a495de355e4917a34b1a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d915b89db1b04cca88c2a495de355e4917a34b1a\">d915b89 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended software-engineering work against the selected project by inspecting artifacts, choosing tools/specialists, modifying code, running commands/tests and revising later actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous repository structure, task ambiguity, file/Git state, tool and command results, provider failures, debugging/test outcomes, context limits and specialist findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next engineering tool/action or delegated specialist and determine when enough work/evidence exists to complete the task. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Operator, or a model-backed mutating specialist within its delegated operational scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/tool schemas; WorkflowEngine/DDAL dispatch; permissions/auto-accept; context manager; local tools; task tracker; specialist registry; sessions/memory; bounded tool rounds. - Closure path: task/current project context → model chooses action/tool → first-party dispatch/enforcement executes against the project → concrete result/error returns → model revises the next action or completes. - Boundary reachability: the ordinary CLI/TUI directly creates the Operator and exposes the full model/tool loop; auto-accept is a supported mode. Model-callable spawnagent is included in the normal provider tool schema and dispatch table. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, the deterministic workflow/permission/tool machinery cannot choose the task-specific engineering sequence or semantic code changes. - Evidence: agents/operator.py; provider.py; tools/init.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: approval is default for tool calls, but a supported authorized auto-accept mode provides the autonomous path; external providers supply inference only.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel mutating specialists can in principle collide in a shared workspace, but the reviewed distribution does not supply a worktree/file-ownership/lock/arbitration path that prevents or resolves those operational collisions and feeds the resolution back into later S1 behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic parallel/pipeline/wave scheduling; ContextBus locking/versioning; context handoff; DAF dependency scheduling; agent limits. - Closure path: not applicable. ContextBus can record duplicate-key conflicts, but no consumer uses the conflict list to arbitrate or alter later allocations/actions; shared-context propagation and DAG sequencing alone do not close S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: spawning many agents and passing outputs among them is topology/communication. The Methodology requires an evidenced interference-specific attenuation relation, which is absent for the actual mutating operational cells. - Evidence: agents/parallel.py; orchestrator/contextbus.py; workflow.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the ContextBus&#39;s “conflict” name is not sufficient; it detects information-key collisions but does not resolve them or control shared-workspace interference.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material discretionary whole-system current-control loop over the active operational population is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: routing, wave membership, concurrency, specialist timeouts, critical blocking gates, background-agent limits, workflow dependencies and command schedules are regulated, but chiefly by preset deterministic rules or task-local parent choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 scope. The main Operator can launch specialists and inspect background statuses, but no normal model-callable path was found to reprioritize, steer, reassign or cancel an existing multi-agent population using a whole-system current picture. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ShadowOrchestrator complexity/route/strategy rules; ParallelCoordinator; blocking-role gates; agent-status registry; fixed concurrency/nesting limits; DAF graph scheduler; explicit scheduler worker. - Closure path: preset routing/stage/gate rules can start/stop stages, but no whole-system current view → discretionary resource/commitment/prioritization judgment → changed live S1 commitments → returned operational-state loop is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-backed Vyasa profile describes orchestration behavior but is not the actor used by the normal ShadowOrchestrator.execute control path. Deterministic workflow control does not inherit S3 ownership from the label “orchestrator”. - Evidence: orchestrator/engine.py; agents/parallel.py; tools/agentspawn.py; scheduler.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model-backed blocking specialists can veto critical work, but that narrower assurance judgment is not a general whole-system S3 allocation/commitment owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge an ordinary engineering result through a separately instantiated, role-restricted model reviewer that directly inspects project evidence and returns severity-tagged findings to the parent coding loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: correctness bugs, security flaws, performance/error-handling defects, insufficient tests, dependency problems and false-positive completion by the ordinary implementation actor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the reviewer child independently decides which observed conditions constitute review findings and their severity from its own read-only model/tool loop; the parent model receives that result and can revise the work or request another review. - Decision owner: the model-backed Reviewer (Dharma) child. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: normal model-facing spawnagent tool; specialist AgentRunner/AgentExecutor; separate ContextBus; Reviewer system prompt; read-only tool policy; foreground result return or background status/result channel; nesting/budget limits. - Closure path: main Operator implements/changes work → autonomously invokes spawnagent(role=&quot;reviewer&quot;, ...) → first-party runtime creates a separate reviewer model loop with direct read/search/Git access → reviewer returns file/line/severity findings as tool result → parent Operator receives the finding in its ordinary transcript → later model turns can fix/retest/review before completion. - Boundary reachability: spawnagent is in the normal provider tool schema and central dispatch, Reviewer is a built-in role, and children are instantiated by first-party code; no external Vyasa fleet or application-authored reviewer is required. Supported auto-accept allows this path without per-call human approval. - Claim being audited: that the parent/current implementation or recent project change is sufficiently correct, secure, performant and tested to be accepted as complete. - Ordinary reporting path: main Operator or mutating specialist&#39;s own tool results and completion response. - Complementary access path: a separately created Reviewer AgentRunner with its own context bus, role prompt and read-only direct repository tools re-inspects project state instead of relying only on the implementer&#39;s narrative. - Independence boundary: reviewer context/role/tool policy is distinct from the ordinary implementer and cannot mutate the workspace; the same underlying provider/model family may be reused, so independence is organizational/session/evidence-path separation rather than provider diversity. - Who acts on findings: the main Operator receives the reviewer response as a normal tool result and owns subsequent corrective code/test/delegation decisions; another reviewer invocation can challenge the revised state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the Reviewer model actor while keeping spawn plumbing and read-only enforcement removes the substantive defect/severity judgment; deterministic machinery cannot reproduce arbitrary code-review findings. - Evidence: provider.py; tools/agentspawn.py; agents/registry.py; agents/operator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: standalone user-invoked /review and deterministic /finish checks are not the basis of the autonomous claim; the positive path is the model-callable reviewer child returned to the active parent agent loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persisted memories, stored specialist results, user-created skills and current task routing can change later prompt context, but they do not form a first-party future-environment sensing → adaptation-option → enacted persistent capability change loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MemoryManager recall; ContextBus/vector result store and retrieval; skill loader/store; provider/model selection; roadmap/timeline and session persistence. - Closure path: prior results/memories are retrieved as later context, and user-created skill files can be loaded, but no standard autonomous path decides to create/adopt a future capability in response to prospective environmental intelligence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: durable experience reuse is memory, and skill creation is a user/project configuration surface. Neither demonstrates the distinct outside-and-then adaptation function. - Evidence: orchestrator/engine.py; skills.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future runs can benefit from stored context; persistence alone is not prospective organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity or ultimate-policy closure is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompts, tool policies, permissions, custom agent/team definitions and blocking rules constrain operation, but no first-party actor adjudicates an identity/ultimate-policy issue and returns that decision as authoritative governing policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S5 scope. - Decision owner: not established inside the runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: main/specialist system prompts; permission manager and auto-accept; agent specs; user-defined Project Studio agents/organisations/flows; config; safety/approval rules. - Closure path: configuration and prompts are supplied/edited externally and then enforced or loaded. No runtime identity-policy issue is escalated to legitimate ultimate authority, decided there and returned to govern subsequent organization-wide operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the primary/specialist agents act inside authored policy; neither they nor deterministic orchestration have authority to redefine DJcode&#39;s ultimate purpose/identity/policy. - Evidence: prompt.py; agents/registry.py; permissions.py; studio.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: custom organizations and approval rules can be durable, but authored configuration is not runtime S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e8b5b127d14fcaaeed6f07855d16ae72aa8c2024/assessments/dj/djcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e8b5b127d14fcaaeed6f07855d16ae72aa8c2024\">e8b5b12 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-16T14:42:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/omarluq/librecode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LibreCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-16\">2026-11-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/omarluq/librecode/tree/1e7ba321306bc5e01a58f4ee9c53f18178f2cb96\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1e7ba321306bc5e01a58f4ee9c53f18178f2cb96\">1e7ba32 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out repository investigation or coding. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tasks differ in relevant files, allowed tools and observed results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a tool, arguments and subsequent actions after results. - Decision owner: The model behind the root or shipped child role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapters, role settings, tool registry, and durable transcript. - Closure path: Tool output is returned as conversation content, shaping the next model action and final response. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves dispatch code without a discretionary coder or explorer. - Evidence: internal/provider/anthropic.go, internal/provider/toolloop.go, internal/agenttask/runtimerunner.go, README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: High - Caveats: A writable child depends on the configured profile. - Boundary reachability: The installed client reaches native provider tool loops and built-in explore and general roles through supported session entry points.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Dampen interference among distinct live operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Independent child agents could contend over one workspace or limited concurrency. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party mechanism selects a response to concrete overlapping task scopes. - Decision owner: The caller or workflow author sets the assignments; the scheduler enforces configured limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent task session isolation, bounded workers, and sequencing primitives. - Closure path: A generic scheduler defers work when a slot is full. It does not route a work-scope collision back to peers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent scheduling and order composition do not supply conflict-specific coordination judgment. - Evidence: internal/agenttask/service.go, internal/workflow/workflow.go, internal/workflowkernel/combinator.go, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate current child or workflow commitments across the owning session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel or durable work can become unnecessary, stalled or in need of replacement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect currently active commitments and select which work to start, continue waiting for, or cancel. - Decision owner: The root model controls its child portfolio. The responsible terminal operator can decide which current workflow remains active. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner-scoped task list, durable status, panel, scheduler and descendant cancellation. - Closure path: Model-triggered cancellation changes child state. Terminal workflow cancellation cascades through the workflow controller and returns its disposition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The respective actor selects a commitment to stop or replace. Cancellation APIs enforce that choice. - Evidence: internal/assistant/agenttool.go, internal/agenttask/service.go, internal/terminal/workflows.go, internal/workflow/service.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: High - Caveats: The two modes operate in supported subsets of the same ownership scope. This does not imply authority over other sessions. - Boundary reachability: The root registers the model-visible agent controls, and the terminal&#39;s workflows panel is wired to the shipped service. - Whole-system current view: The model can list status across its own children. The operator&#39;s session view lists active workflows and shows their child execution details. - Current-control decision scope: Decide which existing child commitments or whole workflow groups to stop, and in the model mode initiate alternate tasks.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently challenge a work claim with complementary evidence and a return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A child can complete with an incorrect change or misleading answer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No packaged independent verdict is routed into rework or acceptance. - Decision owner: The worker controls its ordinary completion. The operator or extension author may compose review. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only exploration, task transcripts, schema checking and workflow APIs. - Closure path: Results return to the delegating context. Structural schema validation can retry formatting. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An explorer is an operational role. Result shape checks do not adjudicate underlying work. - Evidence: README.md, internal/agenttask/runtimerunner.go, internal/workflow/workflow.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Anticipate environmental changes and alter future operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider characteristics and workspace needs may vary across deployments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped decision path selects a future capability alternative from external evidence. - Decision owner: The operator chooses providers and profiles. Trusted extension authors supply their code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider configuration, skills and extension manager. - Closure path: An installation or configuration is consumed by later use. No shipped evidence-driven selection loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task-local retries or selectable startup capabilities do not supply prospective environmental judgment. - Evidence: README.md, README.md, docs/extension-manager.md, internal/agenttask/runtimerunner.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve a standing purpose or authority issue for subsequent LibreCode operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operator and project preferences can differ over tool authority and mutating child roles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped governance path adjudicates an ultimate policy exception for future work. - Decision owner: The operator or project configuration author sets profiles; extensions are separately trusted local code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Profile loading, tool filtering and mutation policy checks. - Closure path: Loaded permissions constrain subsequent task execution and can be externally edited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static authority settings and enforcement constrain operation without closing a governance escalation. - Evidence: README.md, internal/agenttask/runtimerunner.go, internal/agent/catalog.go, docs/extension-runtime.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/854c6e5441709b014c01f803c23311fdf366a354/assessments/li/librecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 854c6e5441709b014c01f803c23311fdf366a354\">854c6e5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:21:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dxiv/dxa-deimos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Deimos</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dxiv/dxa-deimos/tree/933a20c064294cb84dafaae2fe0dae617eb44c48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 933a20c064294cb84dafaae2fe0dae617eb44c48\">933a20c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue uncertain coding outcomes through iterative model-selected file, shell, search and other first-party tool actions, including individual teammate tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete coding instructions, new source state, command/tool outcomes, model/provider errors, changing team work and feedback from other agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next tool/operation or completion after observing the previous tool/agent result. - Decision owner: a configured local/cloud inference model operating in a first-party Deimos query/agent-tool loop; external API only supplies inference and transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK adapter and provider profiles, tool dispatcher, terminal session state, permission gates, context handling, subprocess/file tools and model selection. - Closure path: user or team assignment → model request → selected first-party action/tool → tool output → next model turn/operation or completion returned to user/team lead. - Boundary reachability: the public CLI launcher loads the built native Deimos entrypoint; the own-source query engine and AgentTool produce action/feedback cycles. Team spawning is reachable from the documented first-party optional mode and not from an adjacent hosted agent alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model while leaving API adapters and tools in place removes the context-sensitive decision about coding actions; static runtime mechanisms cannot replace the operational choice. - Evidence: bin/deimos.mjs; src/entrypoints/cli.tsx; src/query.ts; src/QueryEngine.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; src/tools/AgentTool/runAgent.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is evidence for native decision-right mapping, not live benchmark accuracy or successful packaging of every optional tool.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate actual contention and premature succession between distinct active operational teammates through shared task-ownership conflict checks and dependency admission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent attempts by two teammates to claim one task, overcommitment of a busy agent, and a successor task begun before its dependencies finish. - Decisive decision or feedback right: file-lock-serialized, owner/dependency/busy-state checked admission or rejection of a claim; result controls whether a given S1 may start the task. - Decision owner: native deterministic first-party coordination constructor; teammate models may select subsequent eligible work but no separately evidenced autonomous agent owns the conflict-specific arbitration policy. Hence C, not automatic A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proper-lockfile, disk task list, owner/blockedBy graph, watcher, in-process runner, TaskUpdate and mailbox status. - Closure path: teammate sees an unclaimed task → tries claimTask → exclusive lock plus owner/blocked/busy checks → refusal or approved claim returned to watcher/runner → only accepted worker begins its operational prompt; blocked or contending agents wait or inspect newly available work. - Distinct S1 units: two named first-party team workers handling separate repository modifications under a single team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous ownership claims and incompatible active dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party atomic locking and task dependency checks, not merely a shared mailbox or generic queue. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: task-list watcher/in-process runner uses returned success/failure to select or defer its next prompt; failure to submit releases the claim. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implemented relation directly identifies and rejects a concrete conflict between independent operating agents before work proceeds, and delivers that arbitration into the operational selection loop. - Boundary reachability: native DEIMOSEXPERIMENTALAGENTTEAMS/flag opt-in enables TeamCreate and teammate spawn; the shipped useTaskListWatcher and inProcessRunner actually call the same src/utils/tasks.ts claimTask method. No external runtime is needed for the collision rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic lock arbitration remains even if agentic reasoning is removed; it establishes constructed S2 conflict regulation, not model-owned S2 policy. - Evidence: src/utils/tasks.ts; src/hooks/useTaskListWatcher.ts; src/utils/swarm/inProcessRunner.ts; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts; src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: direct manual TaskUpdate ownership editing is distinct from the protected claimTask code; the positive relation is the runtime automatic-claim path, not a universal no-conflicts guarantee.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain cohesion over an opted-in current team by assigning, revising and supervising several ongoing operational obligations and available teammates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked dependent work, idle or overburdened teammates, unexpected subtask failures, stale commitments and competing priorities in the active team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-directed team lead may choose team formation, current owner/priority allocation, updated dependencies, interventions to unblock work, instructions to teammates and shutdown/reallocation of the current team. - Decision owner: first-party team-lead model at the local team recursion in supported opt-in mode; task persistence and mailbox delivery merely enforce/communicate its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamCreate/team file/roster, TaskList/TaskUpdate, SendMessage, worker status and shared task metadata. - Whole-system current view: the team-lead can inspect the complete team task list with owner/status/blocker information and receive teammate completion/blocked or idle messages, rather than merely the contents of one operative tool call. - Current-control decision scope: priority/ownership among several currently operative teammates, resource allocation and intervention through task ownership and communication. - Closure path: teammate blocks/completes or signals difficulty → lead reads whole-team state/messages → model chooses reassignment/dependency adjustment/worker instruction → TaskUpdate/SendMessage records/delivers decision → subsequent teammate activity changes. - Boundary reachability: src/tools/TeamCreateTool/TeamCreateTool.ts initializes native team lead and shared task list in the enabled first-party mode; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts and src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts expose ongoing obligations and discretionary owner/dependency changes; src/tools/SendMessageTool/SendMessageTool.ts returns consequential directions to native teammates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic task store cannot decide what current team obligations should be changed in response to evolving progress; the model controls this whole-current choice rather than only forwarding messages. - Evidence: src/tools/TeamCreateTool/TeamCreateTool.ts; src/tools/TeamCreateTool/prompt.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts; src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts; src/tools/SendMessageTool/SendMessageTool.ts; src/utils/swarm/teamHelpers.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: only the explicitly opt-in team mode supplies whole-current control; default single coder and user approvals are not re-labelled as S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no enabled independent complementary operational-evidence review with a consequential findings-return loop is established in the open Deimos distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source changes and task-completion claims may be wrong, but routine local reviews/tests and maintainer CI do not establish this additional organizational audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor would inspect distinct primary artifacts, judge contradictory evidence and inform current control; the frozen open build instead disables the named specialized verifier and has no completed native alternative audit path. - Decision owner: no positive native S3 owner established. Internal verifier code exists as an adjacent disabled feature; the main coding agent controls ordinary local review. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local /review prompt and /security-review, test/code tools, optional external /ultrareview, and maintainer CodeQL/CI—all outside a first-party independent complementary audit close. - Closure path: ordinary coder may read source or tests and continue S1; no alternative first-party auditor with separate judgment and a returned metasystemic control correction is operationally established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: verificationAgent presence is not sufficient when build-time feature(&#39;VERIFICATIONAGENT&#39;) is false and runtime GrowthBook gate defaults false, and a local model requested to review its own work is not an independent complementary audit by name. - Evidence: scripts/build.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/builtInAgents.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/verificationAgent.ts; src/tools/VerifyPlanExecutionTool/VerifyPlanExecutionTool.ts; src/tools/ReviewArtifactTool/ReviewArtifactTool.ts; src/commands/review.ts; src/commands/security-review.ts; .github/workflows/codeql.yml. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for the frozen open-build boundary. - Caveats: this does not assert that developer CI, an internal build, or a separately hosted external service lacks review/audit. Their decision rights are not credited to this assessed publicly shipped CLI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed externally prospective adaptation mechanism that generates future capability options and returns them into current S3 oversight was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: latency/cost changes among inference providers, new task and repository context and externally hosted service capabilities are addressed by operational routing/configuration rather than a prospective organizational intelligence cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured Python provider sidecar may select a current backend by observed health/latency; user/provider profiles govern model selection. This is not a future-directed adaptation judgment over the team&#39;s capabilities. - Decision owner: no evidenced first-party S4 at the local installed coding/agent-team recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: python/smartrouter.py experimental sidecar, provider bootstrap/discovery, model choice and task planning; PROACTIVE=false and AGENTTRIGGERS=false in scripts/build.ts. - Closure path: current model/backend switch informs the next inference request, not a future-state/options decision returned into S3 control to transform longer-run capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: provider health scoring and context-dependent code plan are operational, not environmental foresight; future-looking labels on disabled features cannot supply an implemented S4 loop. - Evidence: python/smartrouter.py; scripts/provider-recommend.ts; scripts/build.ts; src/commands/ultraplan.tsx; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separately managed user organization may assess future models and alter its installation, but that external governance is not native S4 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party highest-level identity/ultimate policy decision path is established for this assessed local agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credentials, model/provider choice, tool permissions, usage limits and opt-in team access are operational constraints rather than conflicts about organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users/developers choose settings and approve particular tools, while the runtime enforces fixed rules; no identity/policy choice closing S3–S4 tension is observed. - Decision owner: no native ultimate-policy owner; external human or maintainer may govern their broader project independently. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config/profile editor, local permission gates, policy-limit helpers, source build flags and team mode enablement. - Closure path: a local tool action is approved/rejected or a mode toggled and coding continues; no higher-order identity issue → legitimate policy authority → binding returned system-operation constraint is wired as a first-party S5 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static source-build policy and local operator approvals are not an autonomous S5 decision or a legitimate parent-mode ultimate-policy return. - Evidence: src/services/policyLimits/index.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts; scripts/build.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; README.md; LEGAL.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: organizational/legal governance recorded in public docs is adjacent, not automatically an operating parent mode of the Deimos CLI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bd599e834eea44b568ccea9819d5e49adbf7061f/assessments/deimos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bd599e834eea44b568ccea9819d5e49adbf7061f\">bd599e8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:25:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/525300887039/BareAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BareAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/525300887039/BareAgent/tree/1dfd1057d372e872f54c0a666f375dce2bc11a26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1dfd1057d372e872f54c0a666f375dce2bc11a26\">1dfd105 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - Function: Autonomously perform uncertain developer coding tasks through file/shell/tool actions and observed feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository state, errors, test failures, unavailable resources, and follow-up developer questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Root model or each teammate model chooses which action to execute and how to respond to the returned result. - State: A - Decision owner: First-party model-driven root/teammate agent loops; the provider supplies inference while the built-in runtime carries out local actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed tool schemas/handlers, hooks/permissions, retry policy, message persistence/compaction, shell and file tools, background execution. - Closure path: Work request → model-selected action → tool/handler result → model feedback in the same coding loop → next action or final result; background teammate handles a message or claimed task using the same loop. - Boundary reachability: Default REPL invokes native agentloop; the documented /team spawn path creates independent running AutonomousAgent workers that invoke agentloop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool execution and permissions do not pick a context-appropriate coding action without the operative model. - Evidence: core/loop.py; main.py; autonomous.py; tools.py. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - Function: Attenuate duplicate execution of one shared pending task when several autonomous teammate S1 workers see the same ready item. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running teammates can concurrently select the same pending task before either finishes; duplicate claims/work would waste work or conflict on the same task commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The shared task manager compares a task&#39;s expected status against current status and atomically commits the first claim; a rejected claimant re-evaluates the ready set rather than executing that task. - State: C - Decision owner: First-party deterministic claim-and-retry constraint; a developer/agent must still supply any discretionary policy for arbitration or rescheduling beyond first-winner conflict attenuation. No S2 coordinating agent owns adaptive arbitration as shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskManager RLock, expectedstatus compare-and-update, persisted .tasks.json, dependency readiness, per-teammate run loops. - Closure path: Concurrent ready-task observation → first atomic pending→inprogress update → competing update raises ValueError → second teammate skips the lost claim and searches other ready tasks instead. - Boundary reachability: TaskManager instance is shared by the main REPL and spawned AutonomousAgent workers through maketeamhandlers; workers invoke claimtask for ready work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision-sensitive response is embedded in explicit first-party code but chosen by lock/CAS rules, not by a model&#39;s independent coordination judgment, so it supports C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: Separately spawned AutonomousAgent background threads, each with its own provider, prompt, handler set, and task execution loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing acquisition of the same ready persisted task by two running teammate agents. - Attenuating coordination relation: Compare-and-set claim on shared task status and losing-claim rejection within the worker claim loop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Losing worker does not execute a task it failed to claim; it resumes scan or waits, while winning worker marks done/failed on completion. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: This path rejects a concrete competing claim by two operating workers and changes which worker may proceed; the generic message bus, team routing and static workflow DAG are not used as independent S2 evidence. - Evidence: tasks.py update/getreadytasks; AutonomousAgent claim loop; shared TaskManager wiring. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - Function: Own whole-current commitments and interventions for a live team of independently executing coding S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple running teammate commitments, pending/done/failed tasks and developer requests can require reassignment, roster adjustment, work reprioritization, shutdown, or additional work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In agent mode the root model may inspect task and team rosters, create/update tasks, register/spawn/shutdown workers, and send targeted work requests based on responses. In separate parent mode the human does equivalent current intervention via /team and task command surfaces. - State: A(P) - Decision owner: Autonomous root model in first-party agent-tool mode; human operator for the separately exposed command-controlled parent mode. Background manager and TaskManager transport/enforce these decisions but do not autonomously choose current priorities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teamlist, teamregister, teamspawn, teamsend, teamshutdown; tasklist/taskcreate/taskupdate; per-session MessageBus and background worker state; running-thread probe; reply-to-request protocol. - Closure path: Root agent sees whole-team roster/task status and teammate results → chooses new current allocation or intervention → concrete team/task tool action → updated task commitments, active teammate population, or returned worker instruction → subsequent worker execution/feedback. - Boundary reachability: Team and task tool schemas are standard first-party tools registered through core/tools.py and connected by buildhandlers/maketeamhandlers in main.py. /team commands address the same handlers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The root model can decide when to change team composition, who handles a request, or which commitments to create. A deterministic scheduler only enforces claims; a human alternative exists without removing autonomous tool-mode choice. - Whole-system current view: teamlist returns all registered workers with actual running status; tasklist returns current work records and statuses; teamsend and mailbox responses provide subordinate results. - Current-control decision scope: Create/change a task commitment, launch/stop a worker, and send/revise an operational request across a whole current team; this is not solely selecting a single child function call. - Evidence: TEAMTOOLSCHEMAS and task tools; team handlers and /team commands; worker task behavior; task ledger. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium: meaningful current-control reachability is clear; a live multi-worker strategy adaptation trace has not been independently reproduced - Caveats: Default solo mode is only one S1 and does not exercise a team-level S3. Positive S3 is credited to the documented first-party team mode, not the bare presence of a queue or generic model planning.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - Function: No operationally guaranteed complementary independent audit of claims about teammate execution with a return to whole-current control was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A teammate may produce faulty work or a weak plan; ordinary teamrequestreview solicits a model&#39;s opinion on the plan, not a verified independent observation of what the worker actually did. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The root model or human requests a review; a nominated teammate writes an answer. The standard PLANAPPROVAL path does not require sampled operational artifacts or separate protected evidence before an audit verdict is returned. - State: — - Decision owner: Requested reviewer model for plan opinion and the requester for next action; no distinct source-first operational audit authority guaranteed by shipped contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProtocolFSM PLANAPPROVAL request/response, separate provider-backed teammate, shared read-capable tools, per-request prompt wrapper, optional tests and traces. - Closure path: Requester-supplied plan → teammate review prompt → textual verdict → requester. Missing guaranteed independent operational evidence → audit judgment → whole-current management correction circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent worker identity alone is not sufficient for S3; normal plan approval does not establish sufficiently complementary access to operational reality. - Evidence: teamrequestreview; buildincomingprompt; plan approval protocol; team docs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - Function: No first-party external-and-prospective option-modeling function with feedback into whole-current capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project knowledge, user demands or reusable workflows may affect future work, but first-party skill reflection captures previous local actions and user-promoted drafts rather than prospectively modeling external change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reflection model may propose a reusable skill; the human decides whether to promote; neither step demonstrates a distinct outside/future intelligence decision coupled to S3 current control. - State: — - Decision owner: Current coding model for task learning/drafts, operator for promotion; no attributable S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SkillGenerator two-threshold trigger, isolated reflection model run, pending skill store, /skill keep promotion, model/provider configuration. - Closure path: Accumulated local work → LLM reflects and drafts skill → human promotes into later skill catalog. Missing independent externally sensed change, prospective option appraisal, and return into current team strategy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic experiential learning or reusable procedures do not satisfy the Profile&#39;s external-and-future condition by themselves. - Evidence: skillgen.py; runskillreflection and promotion; skill catalog. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - Function: No first-party ultimate organizational identity/constitutional policy arbitration loop is evidenced at the live coding team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permission limits, developer config, workspace rules and team role descriptions constrain actions but do not resolve ultimate purpose/policy conflicts for the team organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human config sets security permissions/roles; root agent may define teammate work roles. Neither is a proven ultimate identity-policy authority with return to the operating whole. - State: — - Decision owner: Human for local permissions and safety preferences; deterministic enforcement for tool operations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionGuard, team role/system-prompt registry, sandbox, approval hooks, config and fail-closed child permissions. - Closure path: User sets local constraint → permission or role handler applies it to a tool/worker. No S3–S4 identity conflict → legitimate ultimate authority → binding policy decision → whole-team return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task permissions, system prompts and teammate role selection remain execution governance, not proof of constitutive identity decisions. - Evidence: permissions; teammate roles; team composition; hooks. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f07cb3f6ee281940aa69e93dd7a06b494ec31be8/assessments/bareagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f07cb3f6ee281940aa69e93dd7a06b494ec31be8\">f07cb3f &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
